WN.lb(28,{"mt_t0RfQnvh3G":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Kit jumps up and down. How can you play with her?","choices":["Jump too","Sit down and look away","Take her shoes"],"answer":0,"hint":"Watch Kit, then do the same thing she does.","explain":"Jumping too copies Kit, and copying is a way to play together."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"You put brick number {n} on the tower. Who goes next?","choices":["Kit","Me again","Nobody"],"answer":0,"hint":"Take turns: me, then Kit.","explain":"After my go, it is Kit's go. Taking turns lets both of us build one tower.","params":{"n":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Kit has the teddy. You want a go. What do you do?","choices":["Ask, \"Can I have a go?\"","Grab it","Hit Kit"],"answer":0,"hint":"Use kind words to ask, not your hands.","explain":"Asking for a go is kind, and Kit can pass the teddy when she is ready. Grabbing makes her sad."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Mia and Kit build one big tower. Are they playing together?","choices":["Yes, together","No, next to each other","No, they are not playing"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is there one tower or two?","explain":"They share one tower and add bricks in turn, so they are playing together."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Mia and Kit each do their own puzzle, side by side. What kind of playing is that?","choices":["Playing next to each other","Playing together","Not playing"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does each one have her own puzzle?","explain":"Each has her own puzzle, so they are playing next to each other. That is OK too."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Kit asks for the red brick. You have a car and a red brick. What do you give her?","choices":["The red brick","The car","Nothing"],"answer":0,"hint":"Listen: which toy did Kit ask for?","explain":"Kit asked for the red brick, so passing her that is the right toy and helps the game."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"You roll the ball to Kit {n} times. She rolls it back each time. Are you playing together?","choices":["Yes","No","Only Kit is playing"],"answer":0,"hint":"The ball goes to Kit and back again.","explain":"Rolling a ball back and forth is a shared game, so we are playing together.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Kit is rolling a ball. You just watch. Does Kit know you want to play?","choices":["No, I need to ask","Yes, she always knows","Yes, if I stare"],"answer":0,"hint":"Kit cannot see inside your head. How can she find out?","explain":"Kit cannot guess what I want. Asking \"Can I play?\" tells her."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Kit claps her hands. How can you play with her?","choices":["Clap too","Walk away","Shout \"Stop!\""],"answer":0,"hint":"Watch what Kit does. Can you do the same?","explain":"Copying a friend is one of the first ways children play together, so clapping too joins Kit's game."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Kit has put {n} bricks on the tower. She just had a go. Whose turn is it now?","choices":["My turn","Kit's turn again","Nobody's turn"],"answer":0,"hint":"Kit just went. Who goes after Kit?","explain":"Taking turns means my go, then Kit's go. Kit just had her go, so now it is my turn.","params":{"n":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Kit says, \"Can I have the ball?\" What do you do?","choices":["Give Kit the ball","Hide the ball","Give Kit the car"],"answer":0,"hint":"Listen to what Kit asked for.","explain":"Kit asked nicely for the ball. Passing her the toy she asked for is a kind way to play together."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Mia and Kit play side by side. Each has {n} crayons and her own picture. Is that OK?","choices":["Yes, it is OK","No, they must share","No, they are cross"],"answer":0,"hint":"Do they look upset, or just busy?","explain":"Playing next to a friend is a normal stage for small children and is how playing together starts.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Kit is playing chase. You want to join. What do you say?","choices":["\"Can I play?\"","Nothing, just push","\"Go away!\""],"answer":0,"hint":"Use kind words to join in.","explain":"Asking \"Can I play?\" lets Kit know you want to join, and she can say yes."}],"explain":{"kid":["You can play next to a friend. You have your toys. Kit has her toys. That is OK.","You can play with a friend too. Kit claps. You clap too! Now you play together.","Build one tower. You put a brick on. Then Kit puts one on. That is taking turns.","Kit asks for the ball. Give it to her. Want to join a game? Ask, \"Can I play?\" Do not grab."],"adult":["This topic, for ages 2 to 3, is about moving from playing alongside other children to playing with them. Most two-year-olds play next to a friend with their own toys and watch what the friend does. This parallel play is a normal stage, not a problem to fix. Over the next year, short shared moments grow from it: copying, swapping a toy, building one thing together, or a simple chase.","In the lab, your child copies what Kit does, takes turns to build one tower, passes Kit the toy she asks for, and chooses how to join her ball game (watch, grab, or ask \"Can I play?\"). Then they roll the ball back and forth and sort pictures into playing next to each other or playing together.","Common mistakes: expecting a two-year-old to share on demand, which leads to tears, and treating side-by-side play as a sign that something is wrong. It is easier to start with turn-taking games that have a clear \"my go, your go\", and to give them words such as \"Can I have a go?\" than to force a toy into another child's hands. Praise the moments when they pass, copy or wait.","Play together at home with one shared thing: one tower, one ball, one jigsaw. Say whose turn it is out loud, and let your child see you ask, wait and say thank you."]},"familyTip":"Roll a ball back and forth with your child and say \"my go, your go\" each time. Then invite a teddy or a sibling to join, and let your child show them how to take turns.","keyIdea":"Young children first play next to each other, each with their own toys, and that is normal. Step by step they start to play together: copying a friend, taking turns, passing a toy, asking \"Can I play?\" and sharing one game such as building a tower or rolling a ball.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Next to or together? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Next to","Together"],"items":[["each has own crayons",0],["each has own puzzle",0],["each has own book",0],["build one tower",1],["roll a ball back and forth",1],["play chase",1],["pass a toy",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagplay","caption":"Flagship lab: copy Kit, take turns on one tower, pass the toy she asks for, ask to join her ball game, then sort next to or together.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_Ag9NSWJu-X":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Leo says, \"It doesn't matter, I don't mind,\" but he does mind. Which style is this?","choices":["Passive","Assertive","Aggressive"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is Leo saying what he really needs?","explain":"Leo hides what he needs, so it is passive. Being quiet and nice is not the same as being assertive."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Someone takes Zoe's seat. She shouts, \"Move, or you'll be sorry!\" Which style is this?","choices":["Aggressive","Assertive","Passive"],"answer":0,"hint":"A threat: is it calm, or does it attack?","explain":"A threat attacks the other person, so it is aggressive, even if Zoe was right to want her seat back."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Sam has asked {n} times to have a turn. Which reply is assertive?","choices":["\"I've asked a few times. Please can I have a turn now?\"","\"Fine, I don't want a turn anyway.\"","\"You're so selfish. Give it to me!\""],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for the reply that is calm, clear and kind.","explain":"The first reply says what Sam needs, calmly. The second gives in (passive) and the third blames and grabs (aggressive).","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Mia says, \"I feel like you are mean.\" Why is this not a good I-statement?","choices":["It is really a blame about the person","It does not start with I","It is too short"],"answer":0,"hint":"Mean is a word about the person. Is it a feeling?","explain":"Mean is not a feeling, it is a label for the other person. A good I-statement names a feeling, like sad, and the action: \"I feel sad when you laugh at me.\""},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Two friends talk it through and agree to take turns on the bike, {n} minutes each. What is the agreement called?","choices":["The resolution","The conflict","The boundary"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is this the problem, or the fix?","explain":"The fix that ends a conflict is the resolution. The conflict was the disagreement about the bike.","params":{"n":[5,15]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Raj says, \"Please ask before you borrow my things.\" What is Raj setting?","choices":["A boundary","A conflict","A compromise"],"answer":0,"hint":"Raj is drawing a line for how others treat his things.","explain":"A boundary is a line you set for how others treat you or your things. Saying it calmly is assertive."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Being assertive means...","choices":["Saying what you need, calmly and with respect","Getting your own way every time","Keeping quiet to stay out of trouble"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the middle of the line from quiet to loud.","explain":"Assertive means you say what you need, calmly, and you respect the other person too. It does not mean you always win."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which word means two sides talking it through to find a deal?","choices":["Negotiate","Mediate","Aggressive"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is there a helper in the middle, or just the two sides?","explain":"To negotiate is for the two sides to talk it through. To mediate is for a third person to help them."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Eli wants the blue team. Kim wants the red team. Which is a compromise?","choices":["Eli plays blue today and red next time","Kim gives in, and Eli gets his way","They both stop playing for good"],"answer":0,"hint":"Do both of them get some of what they want?","explain":"Taking turns means each gives up a little and both get some. One person giving in is not a compromise."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Jo takes Kai's pencil. Kai says, \"That's my pencil. Please give it back.\" Which style is this?","choices":["Assertive","Passive","Aggressive"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is Kai calm? Does he say what he needs?","explain":"Kai is calm and clear, and he says what he needs. That is assertive."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Ruby waits {n} minutes for the swing. Then Max pushes in. Ruby says nothing and walks away. Which style is this?","choices":["Passive","Assertive","Aggressive"],"answer":0,"hint":"Did Ruby say what she needed?","explain":"Ruby went quiet and gave in, so her need was never said. That is passive.","params":{"n":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which one is an I-statement?","choices":["I feel upset when you talk over me.","You always talk over me.","You are so rude."],"answer":0,"hint":"An I-statement starts with I and names a feeling.","explain":"\"I feel upset when you talk over me\" says the feeling and the action, with no blame. The others start with you and blame."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Ava and Ben both want all {n} minutes of break: Ava for tag, Ben for football. Which plan is a compromise?","choices":["Half the break for each game","Ben gives in and plays tag","Ava shouts until Ben agrees"],"answer":0,"hint":"In a compromise, does one person give in, or do both give a little?","explain":"Sharing the time means both give up a little and both get some. If one gives in, that is not a compromise.","params":{"n":[10,30]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Two friends argue. A teacher stands between them and helps each one listen. What is the teacher doing?","choices":["Mediating","Negotiating","Being passive"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is the teacher one of the two sides, or a helper in the middle?","explain":"A helper in the middle who helps both sides listen is mediating. Negotiating is when the two sides talk it out."}],"explain":{"kid":["When someone upsets you, there are three ways to answer. Passive means you go quiet or give in. Aggressive means you shout, blame or threaten. Assertive is in the middle: you say what you need, calmly, and you are kind about it.","Assertive is not the same as loud. Shouting \"Give it back, thief!\" makes the other person feel attacked, so they fight back. Saying \"That's my pencil. Please give it back\" is calm and clear, and it works better. Being quiet and nice is not assertive either, if you never say what you need.","An I-statement is a good way to be assertive. Say how you feel, what they did, and what you need: \"I feel sad when my book gets lost. Could we look for it together?\" Start with I, not you, and talk about what happened, not about what the person is like.","A conflict is a disagreement. You negotiate when you talk it through to find a deal. A helper in the middle can mediate. A compromise is when both of you give up a little, so you both get some of what you want. The fix you agree on is the resolution. A boundary is a line you set, like \"Please ask before you take my things.\""],"adult":["This topic is for ages 7 to 11. It gives children the words for healthy talk when things go wrong: passive, assertive and aggressive styles, I-statements, conflict, negotiate, mediate, compromise, resolution and boundary. With the words, a child can name what happened and choose a better way next time.","The three styles are best taught as one line, from quiet to loud, with assertive in the middle. Passive means the need is never said, so the problem stays. Aggressive means the need is said as an attack, so the other person defends. Assertive means the need is said clearly and with respect for both people. An I-statement (\"I feel ... when ... Could we ...?\") is the usual tool taught in schools.","Common mistakes: thinking assertive means loud or getting your own way; thinking being quiet and nice is the same as being assertive; \"I feel like you are rude\", which is a blame with I in front; using always and never, which start an argument; and thinking a compromise means one person gives in. Children also mix up negotiate (the two sides talk) and mediate (a third person helps).","In the lab your child slides Kai's reply from passive to aggressive and watches Jo react, sorts six replies into the three styles, builds two I-statements from pieces, shares a 20-minute break between tag and football until both get a fair part, and matches five conflict words to scenes."]},"familyTip":"When a squabble comes up, pause and ask: \"Was that passive, assertive or aggressive?\" Then help your child try it again as an I-statement: \"I feel ... when ... Could we ...?\" Praise the calm, clear version, even if it does not get them their way.","keyIdea":"There are three ways to speak up. Passive means you go quiet or give in, so no one hears what you need. Aggressive means you shout, blame or threaten, so the other person feels attacked. Assertive means you say what you need, calmly and with respect. An I-statement helps: \"I feel upset when you talk over me. Could we take turns?\" A conflict is a disagreement. You negotiate by talking it through, a helper can mediate, and a compromise is when both give up a little. The fix you agree on is the resolution.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"I-statement or you-blame? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["I-statement","You-blame"],"items":[["I feel sad when my game gets knocked over.",0],["I feel left out when I'm not picked.",0],["I feel cross when you take my pens.",0],["You always ruin everything.",1],["You are so annoying.",1],["You never listen to me.",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagassert","caption":"Flagship lab: slide a reply from passive to aggressive, sort replies, build I-statements, find a compromise, match conflict words.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_QXnZ5TPCib":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Mo has no toy to play with. You have three cars. What is kind?","choices":["Offer Mo a car","Keep all three cars","Hide the cars"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who has nothing? What could you give them?","explain":"Offer Mo one car. Mo has something to play with, and you still have two."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"You want a turn on the swing. What can you say?","choices":["Can I have a turn next?","Get off, it's mine!","Nothing, just push"],"answer":0,"hint":"Use kind words to ask. Pushing and shouting hurt.","explain":"Ask: can I have a turn next? Then wait. When your friend is done, it is your go."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"You have {n} blocks. You give Kit 1 block to play with. How many blocks do you have now?","answer":"n-1","hint":"Take away the one you gave to Kit.","explain":"{n} blocks take away 1 is {=n-1} blocks. And Kit has 1 block too, so you can both build.","params":{"n":[3,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Kit has 3 biscuits and you have 1. Is that fair?","choices":["No, not the same","Yes, it is fair","Yes, Kit is bigger"],"answer":0,"hint":"Fair means you both have the same number.","explain":"3 and 1 are not the same, so it is not fair. Deal them one each: 2 for Kit and 2 for you."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Pip is having a turn with your ball. You want it back now. What is best?","choices":["Wait for Pip's go to end","Grab it back","Shout at Pip"],"answer":0,"hint":"Pip is only having a turn. Will the ball come back?","explain":"Wait. When Pip's go is done, the ball comes back to you. Grabbing stops Pip's turn."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"{n} children take turns on the slide. Each child has one go. How many goes until everyone has had a go?","answer":"n","hint":"One go for each child. Count the children.","explain":"One go each for {n} children is {n} goes. Then everyone has had a turn.","params":{"n":[3,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"In a game, Kit had a go and then you had a go. Who goes next?","choices":["Kit","Me","The grown-up"],"answer":0,"hint":"My turn, your turn: the go keeps swapping.","explain":"Kit, then you, so next it is Kit again. The turns swap after every go."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"You have the paint pot and your friend has none. What can you say?","choices":["Do you want a turn?","Go away","It's all mine"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about how your friend feels with nothing to paint with.","explain":"Say: do you want a turn? Then you can take turns with the paint, or both use it."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Sharing and taking turns: you just rolled the dice in a game with Kit. Whose turn is it now?","choices":["Kit's turn","My turn again","Nobody's turn"],"answer":0,"hint":"In a game the turn swaps after every go. Who has not just gone?","explain":"You just had your go, so now it is Kit's turn. Then it swaps back to you."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"There are {=2*k} grapes. You share them: one for you, one for Kit. How many grapes does each of you get?","answer":"k","hint":"Deal them out one at a time: one for you, one for Kit, until none are left.","explain":"{=2*k} grapes dealt one each makes {k} for you and {k} for Kit. Same for both, so it is fair.","params":{"k":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"You let Pip have a turn with your teddy. What happens when Pip's go is done?","choices":["The teddy comes back to me","Pip keeps it for ever","The teddy is lost"],"answer":0,"hint":"Having a turn is like borrowing. What happens to things we borrow?","explain":"A turn ends, and then the teddy comes back to you. Sharing is giving a turn, not giving it away."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"In a game, you have {n} goes and Kit has {n} goes. How many goes is that altogether?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Count your goes, then count on Kit's goes.","explain":"{n} goes for you and {n} goes for Kit makes {=2*n} goes altogether. You both got the same number.","params":{"n":[2,5]}}],"explain":{"kid":["In a game, we take turns. I have a go. Then you have a go. Then it is my turn again. Say: my turn, your turn!","When you share your truck, your friend has a turn with it. It does not go away for good. When their go is done, it comes back to you.","Look around. Does a friend have no toy? You can give them one. Say: do you want a turn?","Sharing snacks? Give one to you, then one to your friend. Keep going. Now you both have the same. That is fair."],"adult":["This sits in personal, social and emotional development, under building relationships. The EYFS early learning goal asks children to work and play cooperatively and take turns with others. Development Matters for 3 to 4 year olds names playing with one or more other children and finding solutions to conflicts. The Head Start framework lists cooperative play and turn-taking under relationships with other children. At 3 to 4, expect turn-taking with a grown-up's help, offering a toy now and then, and using the words my turn and your turn.","Three ideas sit underneath. Turn-taking is reciprocity: the go swaps after each move, so each child can predict when their next go comes. Turn-based sharing means lending a toy for a while, which is easier for this age than giving it away, because the toy comes back. Fair sharing is equal shares, made by dealing out one for you and one for me.","Common mistakes: making a child hand a toy over the moment another child asks, which teaches that sharing means losing; expecting a 3-year-old to share well with no help; very long turns, so the waiting child gives up; and praising only the child who gives. Be careful with blame, too. Grabbing at this age is normal. Name the step instead: ask for a turn, then wait for the toy to come back.","In the lab your child taps whose turn it is in a race game, lends Kit a fire truck and watches it come back, offers a toy to the friend who has none, deals out strawberries one each until the plates match, and sorts kind and unkind ways to play."]},"familyTip":"Play a short game with a die or a ball and say each swap out loud: 'My turn… your turn.' When a sibling wants a toy, try 'You can have it when Sam has finished', then make sure it really does come back.","keyIdea":"Taking turns means one person has a go, then the next, then back again: my turn, your turn. Sharing a toy means giving someone a turn with it, and the toy comes back when their go is done. If a friend has nothing to play with, you can offer a toy. When we share out food, one for you and one for me makes it fair, so everyone gets the same.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Sharing or not? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Sharing and turns","Not sharing"],"items":[["Rolling the ball back and forth",0],["Grabbing the bike",1],["Saying: your turn!",0],["Keeping every block",1],["Giving the truck back",0],["Taking two goes in a row",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagshare","caption":"Flagship lab: take turns in a race game, lend a toy and see it come back, offer a toy, share snacks fairly and sort kind play.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_YwE34zDyNz":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which is a kind way to ask to join in?","choices":["\"Can I play too?\"","Push in","Grab the ball"],"answer":0,"hint":"Kind words, said in a calm voice, help others say yes.","explain":"\"Can I play too?\" uses kind words. Pushing and grabbing spoil the game."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"They say \"Not now.\" What does that mean?","choices":["Not yet, maybe later","They will never play with you","They do not like you"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is \"not now\" the same as \"never\"?","explain":"\"Not now\" means not yet. The game may be full, or they may be in the middle of something."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"They say \"Not now.\" What is a good thing to do?","choices":["Find another game","Knock their game over","Shout at them"],"answer":0,"hint":"Pick something calm that does not spoil their game.","explain":"Finding another game is calm and kind. You can ask again later."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Children are playing doctors. Which idea fits the game?","choices":["\"I can be the patient!\"","\"Let's play tag!\"","\"I am the boss!\""],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one is a part in a doctors game?","explain":"A doctor needs a patient, so that idea fits their game. Tag is a new game, and bossing spoils it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Why do you watch before you ask?","choices":["To see what they are playing","To grab a toy fast","To find the boss"],"answer":0,"hint":"In the lab, the apples and coins told Bo something.","explain":"Watching shows you the game, so you can give an idea that fits."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Bo stands near a game but says nothing at all. What can Bo do?","choices":["Ask \"Can I play?\"","Keep quiet all day","Walk off and cry"],"answer":0,"hint":"Do the children know Bo wants to play?","explain":"If Bo says nothing, the children may not know he wants to play. Kind words tell them."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"{n} children play pirates. 2 more children ask \"Can we play?\" and join. How many children play now?","answer":"n+2","hint":"Start at {n} and count on 2 more.","explain":"{n} and 2 more is {=n+2}. The game got bigger because they asked with kind words.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"What comes after you ask \"Can I play?\"","choices":["Give an idea for a part","Watch the game","Go home"],"answer":0,"hint":"The steps are watch, ask, give an idea, play.","explain":"After you ask, give an idea, like \"I can be the shopper!\" Then you play together."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"You ask {n} friends to play. 1 says \"Not now.\" The rest say yes. How many say yes?","answer":"n-1","hint":"Take the 1 who said \"not now\" away from {n}.","explain":"{n} take away 1 is {=n-1}. One \"not now\" is OK, because others may say yes.","params":{"n":[3,6]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"You want to join a game. What do you do first?","choices":["Watch what they are playing","Grab a toy","Shout \"Me! Me!\""],"answer":0,"hint":"Bo looked for clues before he asked. Why did that help?","explain":"Watch first. Then you know the game, and you can ask and give an idea that fits."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"{n} children are playing shops. You ask \"Can I play?\" and they say yes. How many children are playing now?","answer":"n+1","hint":"Start with the {n} children. You join, so count on 1 more.","explain":"{n} and 1 more is {=n+1}. Kind words helped you join the game.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"They are playing trains. Which idea fits their game?","choices":["\"I can be the driver!\"","\"Let's play football!\"","\"Trains are boring.\""],"answer":0,"hint":"Pick a part you can play in their train game.","explain":"A driver is part of the train game, so it shows you want to play with them, not change the game."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"You hear \"Not now.\" You take {n} big, slow breaths, then 1 more. How many breaths is that?","answer":"n+1","hint":"Count the {n} breaths, then add the 1 extra breath.","explain":"{n} and 1 more is {=n+1}. Slow breaths help the sad or cross feeling get smaller.","params":{"n":[2,5]}}],"explain":{"kid":["You see kids playing. You want to play too! First, stop and watch. What are they playing? Who is in the game?","Then use kind words. Say \"Can I play?\" in a calm voice. Do not grab or push in. Grabbing spoils the game.","Give an idea that fits their game. If they play shops, say \"I can be the shopper!\" Now they can see how you fit in.","Some days they say \"Not now.\" That is OK. It does not mean never. Take a big breath. Find a new game, or ask again later."],"adult":["Joining a group that is already playing is one of the hardest social skills for young children. In England it sits in the EYFS prime area Personal, Social and Emotional Development. The early learning goal Building Relationships asks children to work and play cooperatively and take turns with others, and to form friendships with peers. Development Matters suggests helping three- and four-year-olds find solutions to conflicts, for example accepting that not everyone can be Spider-Man in the game and suggesting other ideas. In the US, the Head Start ELOF goals for preschoolers include positive interactions with other children and cooperative play.","Research on group entry (Putallaz and Gottman; Corsaro's work on access rituals) finds the same pattern. Children who join well watch or hover first, then make a comment or offer that fits what the group is already doing. Children who are turned away more often call attention to themselves, change the game or barge in. Key words for this age: watch, ask, idea, join in, take turns, and the phrase \"not now\".","Common mistakes: grabbing a toy or pushing in, shouting \"Me! Me!\", trying to change the game (\"Let's play football instead\"), or hovering silently and never asking. Be careful with the other big slip too: hearing \"not now\" as \"they hate me\" or \"never\", and then giving up or spoiling the game. First refusals are very common, even for well-liked children, and a calm second try or a different group often works.","In the lab your child watches a game of shops and taps the clues that show the game. They sort kind and unkind ways to ask, and drag an idea that fits into three different games. They help Bo the bear after \"not now\" by picking calm next steps, and put watch, ask, give an idea and play in order. Every drag has a big button twin."]},"familyTip":"At the park, watch a group together for a moment first and whisper, \"What are they playing? What part could you be?\" Practise \"Can I play?\" with a soft toy at home, and sometimes have the toy say \"Not now\" so your child can try a calm next step.","keyIdea":"To join a game, watch first to see what the children are playing. Then ask with kind words, like \"Can I play?\", and give an idea for a part in their game. If they say \"Not now\", stay calm. Find something else to do, or ask again later.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"They said \"Not now.\" Good next step or not? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["👍 Good next step","👎 Not a good idea"],"items":[["Find another game",0],["Knock their tower over",1],["Ask again a bit later",0],["Shout \"You are mean!\"",1],["Ask a different friend",0],["Snatch their toy",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagentry","caption":"Flagship lab: watch a game and find the clues, sort ways to ask, give an idea that fits three games, help Bo after \"not now\", and order the join-in steps.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_NS5t-Jzlh8":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What does cooperate mean?","choices":["Work together on one job","Do the job all on your own","Tell everyone what to do"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of Fox and Bear lifting the big log.","explain":"To cooperate is to work together on one job, like two friends lifting a log that one friend cannot lift."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"What is a compromise?","choices":["Each friend gets some of what they want","One friend gets their way","Nobody gets anything"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the sign painted half red and half blue.","explain":"A compromise is a fair plan: each friend gives a little and gets a part of what they wanted."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Owl is telling Bear an idea. Which shows Bear is listening?","choices":["Bear looks at Owl and waits","Bear plays with a toy","Bear talks over Owl"],"answer":0,"hint":"Listening signs: look, wait, then say back what you heard.","explain":"Looking at Owl and waiting shows Bear is listening. Being quiet with a toy is not the same as listening."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"You do not like your friend's idea. Which is a kind way to disagree?","choices":["\"I see it a different way.\"","\"Your idea is silly!\"","\"No! Mine is best!\""],"answer":0,"hint":"You can say what you think and still be kind.","explain":"\"I see it a different way\" tells your friend what you think, calmly. Calling an idea silly hurts."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A new child stands on her own at play time. How can you include her?","choices":["Ask her to join your game","Let her watch from far away","Say the game is full"],"answer":0,"hint":"Include means asking someone in, so they are not left out.","explain":"Asking her to join includes her. Watching from far away still leaves her out."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"After Owl talks, Bear says, \"You want to build a bridge?\" Why does Bear say that?","choices":["To show he heard Owl's idea","To change the idea","To end the talk"],"answer":0,"hint":"Saying back what you heard is a listening sign.","explain":"Saying it back shows Owl that Bear really heard the idea. It is part of active listening."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"{n} friends are in a team. 2 friends are on their own, so the team asks them to join. How many are in the team now?","answer":"n+2","hint":"Start at {n} and count on 2 more.","explain":"{n} and 2 more is {=n+2}. The team included the 2 friends, so nobody is left out.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Fox wants to paint. Owl wants to build. Fox shouts until Owl gives up. Is that a compromise?","choices":["No, only Fox got his way","Yes, because they stopped arguing","Yes, because Owl agreed"],"answer":0,"hint":"Did each friend get some of what they wanted?","explain":"No. Owl gave in, so only Fox got his way. A compromise gives each friend a part, like paint first, then build."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"Fox and Owl make a compromise. They play Fox's game for {n} minutes, then Owl's game for {n} minutes. How many minutes do they play in all?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Add Fox's {n} minutes and Owl's {n} minutes.","explain":"{n} + {n} = {=2*n} minutes. Each friend got some of what they wanted, so it is a compromise.","params":{"n":[2,5]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Fox and Owl paint a sign together. Fox paints one half and Owl paints the other half. Which word is that?","choices":["Cooperate","Include","Disagree"],"answer":0,"hint":"They are working on one job together. Which word means that?","explain":"They work together on one job, so they cooperate. Include would mean asking a new friend to join."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"{n} friends build a den. 1 friend is on her own, and they ask her to join. How many friends build the den now?","answer":"n+1","hint":"Start with the {n} friends. Asking her to join adds 1 more.","explain":"{n} and 1 more is {=n+1}. Asking her to join means they include her, so nobody is left out.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Mia wants to play tag. Sam wants to play ball. Which plan is a compromise?","choices":["Play tag first, then ball","Only tag, and Sam must agree","Nobody plays at all"],"answer":0,"hint":"In a compromise, each friend gets some of what they want.","explain":"Tag first, then ball gives each friend a part. If only Mia gets her way, Sam just gives in."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"{n} friends take turns on the slide. Each friend has 1 turn, then the next friend goes. How many turns is that?","answer":"n","hint":"Count 1 turn for each of the {n} friends.","explain":"Each of the {n} friends has 1 turn, so that is {=n} turns. Taking turns is one way a team cooperates.","params":{"n":[2,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A team is a group that works on one job. When we work together, we cooperate. Two friends can lift a big log that one friend cannot lift.","Some days friends want different things. Fox wants red. Owl wants blue. A compromise is a plan where each friend gets a part. They paint it half red and half blue. If only Fox gets his way, that is not a compromise.","To listen, look at the one who is talking. Wait until they stop. Then say back what you heard: \"You want a bridge?\" Now they know you heard them.","It is OK not to agree. Say it in a kind way: \"I see it a different way.\" Do not say \"Your idea is silly.\" And look out for friends on their own. Ask them to join in. That is how we include them."],"adult":["This lesson gives children the words for the habits that friendships and group work depend on. In England, the statutory Relationships Education guidance for primary schools asks pupils to learn about caring friendships and respectful relationships, including how to manage conflict and how to seek help. In the early years, the EYFS goal Building Relationships asks children to work and play cooperatively and take turns with others. The CASEL framework calls these relationship skills: listening well, cooperating, and resolving conflict constructively.","Key words for ages 5 to 8: team, cooperate, share, take turns, compromise, listen, active listening, agree, disagree respectfully, and include. Active listening means facing the speaker, not interrupting, and responding to what was actually said. A compromise means both sides give a little so that each gets part of what they wanted.","Common mistakes: thinking compromise means giving in, so the quieter child always loses out. Mixing up cooperate (working together on one job) with include (inviting someone in). Thinking that being quiet is the same as listening, even while playing with a toy. And thinking that disagreeing is always unkind, so children either stay silent or say \"Your idea is silly.\"","In the lab your child drags Fox and Bear to lift a log neither can lift alone. They choose fair plans when Fox and Owl want different things, and tap the signs that show Bear is listening. They sort kind and unkind ways to disagree, and drag Duck and Mouse into the team so the tower grows. Every drag has a big button twin."]},"familyTip":"Next time two of you want different things, such as which film to watch, say \"Let's find a compromise\" and plan it together, like half of each or one now and one next time. Afterwards, ask your child: \"Did we both get some of what we wanted?\"","keyIdea":"Team words name the things good friends do together. Cooperate means working together on one job. Compromise means each friend gets some of what they want. Listen means look at the talker, wait, and say back what you heard. You can disagree kindly, with calm words. Include means asking others to join, so nobody is left out.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which team word fits? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["🤝 Cooperate","⚖️ Compromise","🙋 Include"],"items":[["Two friends carry a heavy bag together",0],["We all tidy the room so it is done fast",0],["You pick the game today, I pick tomorrow",1],["Half red and half blue, so we both like it",1],["\"Come and play with us!\"",2],["Giving the new child a job in the game",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagteam","caption":"Flagship lab: lift a log together, find fair plans, spot real listening, sort kind ways to disagree, and include friends in the team.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_w4nSIDhIgC":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is a role in a group?","choices":["The job you do in the group","The name of the group","The prize the group wins"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the badges in the lab: crown, light bulb, helping hands and dove.","explain":"A role is your job in the group, like leader, ideas person, supporter or peacemaker."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Four friends all want to be the leader. What will most likely happen?","choices":["They argue, and little gets done","They finish very fast","Everyone is happy"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the treehouse when every friend said “My way!”","explain":"Four leaders give four different orders, so nobody builds. One leader is enough, and the others do other jobs."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Jo holds the box steady and says, “Well done!” Which role is Jo doing?","choices":["Supporter","Leader","Ideas person"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who helps build and cheers the others on?","explain":"Helping and cheering is the supporter’s job. The supporter makes the plan real."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Kai says, “First we get the paper, then we cut. Sam, you hold the glue.” Which role is that?","choices":["Leader","Peacemaker","Supporter"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who makes the plan and gives out the jobs?","explain":"Making the plan and giving out jobs is what a leader does."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Is a supporter less important than the leader?","choices":["No, the group needs every role","Yes, only the leader matters","Yes, supporters just watch"],"answer":0,"hint":"What happened in the lab when the supporter sat out?","explain":"No. Without a supporter, nobody holds the planks and nothing gets built. Every role counts."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Your group picks Mia’s idea, not yours. What helps the group most?","choices":["Help build Mia’s idea","Stop helping until they pick yours","Keep saying your idea is better"],"answer":0,"hint":"You do not always have to be in charge, or have your idea win.","explain":"Helping with the chosen idea is a real job: you are being a supporter. Pushing your idea again stops the work."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"{n} friends all want to lead. Only 1 can be the leader. How many friends need to pick a different role?","answer":"n-1","hint":"One friend leads. Count the rest.","explain":"{n} take away the 1 leader is {=n-1}. Those {=n-1} friends can do ideas, help or keep the peace.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"A team has a leader, a supporter and a peacemaker, but they cannot think what to make. Which role is missing?","choices":["Ideas person","Leader","Peacemaker"],"answer":0,"hint":"They are stuck for something new to try. Who thinks of new things?","explain":"The ideas person is missing. When a group is stuck, ask which job nobody is doing."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"There are {n} groups in a class. Each group has 1 peacemaker. How many peacemakers are there?","answer":"n","hint":"Count 1 peacemaker for each of the {n} groups.","explain":"1 peacemaker in each of {n} groups makes {=n} peacemakers, so every group has someone to help when friends disagree.","params":{"n":[2,6]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Leo says, “What if we make the den into a pirate ship?” Which role is Leo doing?","choices":["Ideas person","Peacemaker","Supporter"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is Leo making a plan, thinking of something new, helping, or calming an argument?","explain":"A pirate ship is a new thing to try, so Leo is the ideas person. A peacemaker helps friends who do not agree."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A team has {n} friends. Only 1 friend is the leader. How many friends do other jobs?","answer":"n-1","hint":"Take the 1 leader away from the {n} friends.","explain":"{n} take away 1 is {=n-1}. Those {=n-1} friends can be the ideas person, supporters or the peacemaker.","params":{"n":[3,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Ava and Ben both want to go first and start to shout. What does a peacemaker do?","choices":["Help each one say what they want, then find a fix","Say that Ava is right","Tell them both to go away"],"answer":0,"hint":"A peacemaker, or mediator, does not pick a winner.","explain":"The peacemaker helps both sides be heard, then helps them find a fix, like taking turns. Picking Ava as the winner is not mediating."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A team needs 4 roles: leader, ideas person, supporter and peacemaker. {n} of the roles have someone. How many roles still need someone?","answer":"4-n","hint":"Start with 4 roles and take away the {n} that are done.","explain":"4 take away {n} is {=4-n}. The team should find someone for each empty role so no job is missed.","params":{"n":[0,3]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A group is a team that works on one job. In a good group, each friend has a role. A role is the job you do in the group.","The leader keeps the plan. She says, “Floor first, then walls.” The ideas person says, “What if we add a ladder?” The supporter holds the plank and says, “Great job!” The peacemaker helps when friends do not agree. Another word for peacemaker is mediator.","What if all four friends want to lead? They all say, “My way!” Nobody builds, so nothing gets done. One leader is enough. The others pick a different job.","Every role counts. With no supporter, nobody holds the planks. With no peacemaker, a fight stops the work. You do not always have to be in charge. Some days you lead, and some days you help a friend’s idea."],"adult":["This lesson teaches the main roles children take in group work and why a group needs a mix of them. It sits in the Interactions strand of the IB PYP personal, social and physical education scope, and in the CASEL relationship skills of teamwork, collaborative problem-solving and showing leadership in groups. The PSHE Association programme of study for key stage 2 also asks pupils to work collaboratively and to resolve disagreements.","Key terms for ages 7 to 9: group, role, leader, ideas person (or idea-generator), supporter (sometimes called an encourager), peacemaker or mediator, take turns, compromise, and cooperate. Many classrooms use set cooperative-learning roles, such as facilitator, recorder, timekeeper and encourager. The names differ, but the idea is the same: each member has a clear job.","Common mistakes: thinking the leader is the most important person, so every child competes for it and the supporting jobs feel like losing. Thinking a mediator decides who is right, when a mediator helps both sides say what they want and then look for a fix both can accept. And thinking a role is fixed for life, rather than something a child can change from one task to the next.","In the lab your child hands out badges to four friends who all want to lead, until one leader is left and the treehouse is built. They match what friends say to each role, fix three stuck teams by adding the missing role, and take one role away at a time to see what breaks. Then they pick their own favourite role and read its strength and its watch-out. Every drag has a big button twin."]},"familyTip":"Next time you cook or tidy up together, give out roles first: one leader who keeps the plan, one ideas person, one helper and one peacemaker. Swap roles next time, then ask your child which role felt best and why.","keyIdea":"People in a group can do different jobs, called roles. The leader keeps the plan and gives out jobs. The ideas person thinks of new things to try. The supporter helps build and cheers others on. The peacemaker, or mediator, helps both sides be heard and find a fix. A group works best with a mix of roles, not with everyone trying to lead.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which role is this friend doing? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["👑 Leader","💡 Ideas person","🤝 Supporter","🕊️ Peacemaker"],"items":[["“First the floor, then the walls.”",0],["Gives each friend a job",0],["“What if we add a slide?”",1],["Thinks of a new way to fix it",1],["Holds the plank steady",2],["“Great job! Keep going!”",2],["“You talk first, then you.”",3],["Helps two friends find a fix",3]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagroles","caption":"Flagship lab: hand out badges to a team of leaders, match roles, add the missing one, and see what breaks without each.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_HBcvu0UxYe":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which one is being a good friend?","choices":["Ella, who lets new children join","Max, who says: do what I say!","Jo, who only plays if you give her sweets"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for kind, fair and letting others join.","explain":"Ella lets others join, which is kind. Max is bossy, and a friend should not make you pay to play like Jo."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"You and your friend had a big fall-out. Can you still be friends?","choices":["Yes, you can make up","No, never again","Only if they say sorry first"],"answer":0,"hint":"All friends fall out sometimes. What can you do to fix it?","explain":"Friends have ups and downs. Calm down, say sorry and play again. You do not have to wait for them to go first."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"You said you would play with Sam at break. Then Ben asks you to play. What does a good friend do?","choices":["Keep the promise and play with Sam","Go with Ben and say nothing","Hide from both of them"],"answer":0,"hint":"You made a promise. What does a friend you can count on do?","explain":"Keeping your promise means Sam can trust you. You could ask Ben to join you both."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"A friend has {n} stickers and shares {k} with you. How many does your friend have left?","answer":"n-k","where":"k<n","hint":"Start with {n} stickers and take away the {k} your friend gave you.","explain":"{n} take away {k} is {=n-k}, so your friend has {=n-k} stickers left after sharing.","params":{"n":[5,10],"k":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A boy whispers and points at a girl, then laughs. Is he being a good friend?","choices":["No, that is mean","Yes, it is only a joke","Yes, if his friends laugh too"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about how the girl feels when someone points and laughs at her.","explain":"Whispering, pointing and laughing at someone is mean. It makes her feel sad and left out."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Kim calls you a mean name, then says sorry. What could you do?","choices":["Say thank you for saying sorry, and play again","Call her a mean name back","Tell everyone not to play with her"],"answer":0,"hint":"She said sorry. How do friends make up?","explain":"Saying sorry is how friends fix a fall-out. Accepting it lets you play again. Being mean back keeps the fall-out going."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which word means a friend who keeps their promises?","choices":["Someone you can count on","Bossy","Sneaky"],"answer":0,"hint":"If they say they will do it, do they do it?","explain":"A friend you can count on does what they said. Bossy means always telling others what to do."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"{a} friends are playing tag. {b} of them have to go home. How many are still playing?","answer":"a-b","where":"b<a","hint":"Start with {a} friends and take away the {b} who went home.","explain":"{a} take away {b} is {=a-b}, so {=a-b} friends are still playing tag.","params":{"a":[4,9],"b":[1,3]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"You want to play blocks. Your friend wants to play tag. What does a good friend do?","choices":["Take turns: tag first, then blocks","Shout until they play blocks","Go off and play alone in a huff"],"answer":0,"hint":"A good friend is fair. How can you both get a go?","explain":"Taking turns to choose is fair, so you both get to play your game. Shouting to get your way is bossy."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"{a} children are playing a game. {b} more children ask to join, and the group says yes. How many children are playing now?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Good friends let others join. Add the new children to the group.","explain":"{a} children and {b} more who joined makes {=a+b} children playing together.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"You broke your friend's crayon by mistake. What does a good friend do?","choices":["Tell them and say sorry","Hide it and say nothing","Say someone else did it"],"answer":0,"hint":"A good friend is honest, even after a mistake.","explain":"Telling the truth and saying sorry is honest. Your friend can trust you, and you can sort it out together."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"You and your friend take turns to choose the game. There are {n} turns at playtime. How many turns do you get to choose?","answer":"n/2","where":"n%2==0","hint":"Turns go me, you, me, you. Split the turns into two fair groups.","explain":"{n} turns shared fairly between two friends is {=n/2} turns each.","params":{"n":[2,10]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A good friend is kind. They say nice things and help you. A good friend lets you join in the game.","A good friend is fair. If you want different games, you take turns to choose. Bossy means you always get your way. That is not fair.","A good friend tells the truth, even after a mistake. They keep their promises. If they say they will save you a seat, they do.","Friends fall out sometimes. That is OK. Take a big breath. Say sorry. Then play again. You can still be friends."],"adult":["This topic is the 'Caring friendships' strand of the DfE guidance for Relationships Education (2019). Pupils learn what good friendships are like: respect, truth, trust, loyalty, kindness and sharing. They also learn that most friendships have ups and downs, and that these can often be worked through. The PSHE Association Key Stage 1 programme and the IB PYP strand on interactions set the same goals for ages 5 to 7.","At this age a friend is often whoever a child is playing with right now. So the lesson names the qualities that make a friendship last. These are kindness, honesty, keeping promises (being reliable), fairness (taking turns to choose) and letting others join (inclusion). It also names unfriendly acts a child can spot: being bossy, unkind words and leaving people out.","Common mistakes: thinking one falling out ends a friendship for good; treating friendship as a deal, as in 'you can play if you give me your car'; thinking a good friend always agrees with you; and fibbing to dodge trouble after an accident. Be careful not to call a child a bad friend. Talk about what they did, and show how to repair it: calm down, say sorry, then play again.","In the lab your child pins good-friend badges on a friend. They choose fair and kind moves when games clash or someone asks to join. They own up and keep a promise, put the steps of making up in order, and spot the unfriendly picture in a strip."]},"familyTip":"Over tea, ask: \"Who was a good friend to you today? What did they do?\" Then ask: \"When were you a good friend?\" Name the quality, such as kind, honest or fair.","keyIdea":"A good friend is kind, honest, fair and someone you can count on: they keep promises and let others join in. Being bossy, saying mean things and leaving people out are not friendly. All friends fall out sometimes. You can make up: calm down, say sorry, then play again.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Good friend or not friendly? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Good friend","Not friendly"],"items":[["Takes turns choosing",0],["Always bossy",1],["Keeps a promise",0],["Says you can't play",1],["Tells the truth",0],["Says mean words",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagfriend","caption":"Flagship lab: pin good-friend badges, choose fair and honest moves, put the steps of making up in order, and spot unfriendly behaviour.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_u1lNk1HXNo":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"You took Kai's crayon. What fixes it?","choices":["Say sorry and give it back","Say sorry and keep it","Hide it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Sorry is the start. What does Kai need back?","explain":"Saying sorry is good, but Kai needs his crayon. Giving it back puts it right."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"You spilled Raj's juice. What helps most?","choices":["Say sorry and help wipe it up","Say: The cup did it!","Walk away"],"answer":0,"hint":"What would make Raj feel better about the mess?","explain":"Saying sorry and wiping up the juice helps fix the oops. Blaming the cup is a fib."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Mia said sorry and helped fix your tower. What can you say?","choices":["That is OK. Let us play!","Go away for ever!","Say sorry ten more times!"],"answer":0,"hint":"Mia said sorry and she helped. How do you make up?","explain":"One real sorry is enough. Saying 'That is OK' lets you both play again."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"You said Jo's picture is ugly. Jo is sad. What helps?","choices":["Say sorry and say something kind about it","Say it again louder","Say nothing"],"answer":0,"hint":"Words can hurt too. What words could help instead?","explain":"Mean words hurt. Say sorry, then say something kind, like 'I like the big sun.'"},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"{a} children got hurt in the game. You have said sorry to {b} of them. How many more need a sorry?","answer":"a-b","where":"b<a","hint":"Everyone who got hurt needs a sorry. Take away the ones you have done.","explain":"{a} take away {b} is {=a-b}, so {=a-b} more children need a sorry from you.","params":{"a":[2,5],"b":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which child needs a sorry?","choices":["Ana, who you bumped by mistake","Kai, who is happy on the swing","Ella, who is reading a book"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for the child who got hurt.","explain":"Ana got hurt, even though it was a mistake, so she needs a sorry. Kai and Ella are fine."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Mia's tower had {n} blocks. It fell. You and Mia build {k} blocks back. How many blocks tall is the tower now?","answer":"k","where":"k<=n","hint":"Count only the blocks that are back on the tower.","explain":"You built {k} blocks back, so the tower is {k} blocks tall now. Mia has {=n-k} more to go.","params":{"n":[3,6],"k":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Ben shouts 'sorry!' and runs off. Mia's tower is still on the floor. Is that enough?","choices":["No. Stay and help build it again.","Yes. He said the word.","Yes, if he says it fast"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is the tower fixed? Is Mia still sad?","explain":"A sorry is the start. Ben should stay and help Mia build the tower again."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"You knocked down Mia's tower. Which is the best sorry?","choices":["Sorry, Mia. I knocked your tower down.","It fell by itself!","Ha ha, it fell!"],"answer":0,"hint":"A good sorry says what you did. Which one does that?","explain":"Saying what you did shows Mia that you know what went wrong, and that you mean your sorry."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"You knocked over a tower of {n} blocks. You help build it again. You put back {k} blocks. How many blocks are still on the floor?","answer":"n-k","where":"k<n","hint":"Start with {n} blocks on the floor. Take away the {k} you put back.","explain":"{n} take away {k} is {=n-k}, so {=n-k} blocks are still on the floor. Keep helping until the tower is back up.","params":{"n":[3,6],"k":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"You bumped Zoe by mistake. Her knee hurts. What do you do?","choices":["Say sorry and get a grown-up","Nothing. It was a mistake.","Say: You are fine, get up"],"answer":0,"hint":"Zoe is still hurt, even if you did not mean it.","explain":"A mistake can still hurt. Say sorry, then help: get a grown-up or a plaster for her knee."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"You took {n} of Leo's cars. You give {k} back. How many more cars do you need to give back?","answer":"n-k","where":"k<n","hint":"Leo needs all {n} cars back. You gave {k}. How many are left to give?","explain":"{n} take away {k} is {=n-k}. Give back {=n-k} more cars so Leo has all of his cars again.","params":{"n":[2,5],"k":[1,3]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Oops! Ben's ball hits Mia's tower. It falls down. Mia cries. Ben did not mean to do it. But Mia is still sad.","Ben says, \"Sorry, Mia. I knocked your tower down.\" He says what he did. That shows he means it. Mia is still a bit sad. So Ben helps. They build the tower again, block by block. Now Mia smiles.","Each oops has its own fix. Took a toy? Give it back. Bumped a friend? Get a grown-up or a plaster. Said a mean word? Say a kind one.","When a friend says sorry to you, you can say, \"That is OK.\" Then you can play again."],"adult":["This topic sits in Personal, Social and Emotional Development in the EYFS (Development Matters 2023 and the Early Learning Goals 'Building Relationships' and 'Managing Self'). It also links to the 'Respectful relationships' and 'Caring friendships' strands of DfE Relationships Education (2019) at Key Stage 1. At 4 to 5 years, children say sorry when a grown-up prompts them. The goal is that they also try to make amends: do something that repairs the harm.","A full apology for this age has three parts. First, name what you did: 'I knocked your tower down.' Second, say sorry. Third, offer a repair that fits the harm. Rebuild what was broken, return what was taken, fetch help for a hurt, or swap a hurtful word for a kind one. Many settings use this restorative approach in place of a bare 'say sorry'. The child who was hurt can then accept the apology, which is the start of forgiveness and playing together again.","Common mistakes: thinking an accident needs no sorry (a hurt is still a hurt, whatever the intent); a quick 'sorry' shouted while running off; saying sorry but keeping the toy; and thinking a sorry must be repeated over and over before you can make up. Be careful not to force a child to say a sorry they cannot yet feel. Calm first, then help them notice the other child's face, then model the words.","In the lab your child picks the sorry that names what was done. They drag the right fix-it to a sad friend: blocks, the teddy or a plaster. Then they find every child in a playground who got hurt, mend hurt words and a spill, and choose how to accept a sorry and play again."]},"familyTip":"Next time there is a toy-room oops, try the three steps together: \"What happened? Say sorry and say what you did. How can we fix it?\" Praise the fix, not just the word sorry.","keyIdea":"When you hurt or upset someone, even by accident, say sorry and say what you did: \"Sorry, Mia. I knocked your tower down.\" Then do something to put it right: build the tower again, give the toy back, or get help for a bump. When someone says sorry to you, you can say \"That is OK\" and play again.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does it help make up? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Helps make up","Does not help"],"items":[["Sorry, I took your car",0],["Give the car back",0],["Run away",1],["Build the tower again",0],["Say: It was not me!",1],["Laugh at the mess",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagsorry","caption":"Flagship lab: pick a good sorry, drag the right fix-it to a sad friend, say sorry to everyone who got hurt, and make up.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_iWGnyUyN2j":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Leo says: I want tag because we played football yesterday. Which reply shows you listened?","choices":["So you want tag because we had football yesterday?","But I want football!","That is a silly reason."],"answer":0,"hint":"Which reply says back Leo's reason?","explain":"Saying back his reason shows Leo you heard him. Then he is more ready to hear you too."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Mo and Jen both want to be the goalie. Which fix can they both accept?","choices":["Swap at half time","Mo is goalie because he asked first","Nobody is goalie"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the fix where both get some of what they want.","explain":"Swapping at half time gives both a turn. Mo always winning leaves Jen with nothing, and nobody being goalie is bad for both."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Sam always lets Ali choose, so they never argue. Is that a fair fix?","choices":["No: Sam should get a say too","Yes: not arguing is all that matters","Yes: Ali is louder"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does Sam get anything he wants?","explain":"Always giving in keeps the peace but is not fair to Sam. A good fix gives both friends a say."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which one needs a grown-up straight away?","choices":["Someone is hitting","Both want the blue pen","You want different games"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is anyone getting hurt, or could they be?","explain":"Hitting is never OK and someone could get hurt, so get a grown-up. Pens and games you can usually sort out together."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Ava and Leo take turns on the swing: {k} pushes each. Ava has had her {k} pushes. How many pushes does Leo get now?","answer":"k","hint":"Taking turns fairly means the same number each.","explain":"Fair turns mean the same each, so Leo gets {k} pushes too. Then it is Ava's turn again.","params":{"k":[3,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Ava says: You always ruin everything! What would be a better way to say it?","choices":["I feel cross because I wanted a turn to choose.","You ruin everything, and I mean it.","Say nothing and sulk."],"answer":0,"hint":"Start with I feel, then give the reason.","explain":"An I-statement tells Leo how you feel and why, without blaming him, so he does not need to fight back."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"While Leo explains his side, what should Ava do?","choices":["Listen to the end, then say back what he said","Plan what she will say next","Talk over him so she is heard"],"answer":0,"hint":"How can Ava show she really heard him?","explain":"Listening to the end and saying back his reason shows Leo she heard him. Planning your reply means you miss what he says."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"Ava and Leo try {t} fixes. {w} of them give one friend everything and the other nothing. The rest give both enough. How many fixes can they both accept?","answer":"t-w","where":"w<t","hint":"A fix that gives one friend nothing will not be accepted. Take those away.","explain":"{t} take away {w} is {=t-w}. Only the {=t-w} fixes that give both friends enough are ones they can both accept.","params":{"t":[3,6],"w":[1,2]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Ava and Leo are shouting about which game to play. What should they do first?","choices":["Calm down, with slow breaths or a short break","Keep shouting until one gives in","Ask everyone to pick a side"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can you think of a good fix while you are very cross?","explain":"When you are very cross it is hard to think. Calming down first makes it possible to talk and listen."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Ava takes {b} slow breaths. Each breath brings her cross-o-meter down 2 points. How many points does it go down?","answer":"2*b","hint":"Each breath is 2 points. Add 2 for every breath, or multiply.","explain":"{b} breaths times 2 points is {=2*b} points. Slow breaths really do help your body calm down.","params":{"b":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which is an I-statement that does not blame?","choices":["I feel left out because I did not get the ball.","You never pass to me!","You are the worst player ever."],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for the one that starts with how I feel and gives a reason.","explain":"I feel left out because ... says your feeling and the reason. It does not blame, so your friend can listen."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Break is {n} minutes. Ava and Leo agree to split it fairly: tag for half, then football for half. How many minutes of tag do they play?","answer":"n/2","where":"n%2==0","hint":"Half of the break is tag. Halve {n}.","explain":"Half of {n} is {=n/2}, so they play tag for {=n/2} minutes, then football for {=n/2}. Both get enough.","params":{"n":[10,30]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Ava wants football. Leo wants tag. They both get cross and start to shout. Shouting will not fix it. First they need to calm down. Take some slow breaths, count to five, or step back for a minute.","Next, Ava says how she feels and why. She starts with I: \"I feel cross because we never play my game.\" That is called an I-statement. It does not blame Leo, so he does not need to fight back.","Then Ava listens to Leo all the way to the end. He says, \"We played football yesterday.\" Ava says it back: \"So you want tag because we had football yesterday?\" Now Leo knows she heard him.","Last, they look for a fix that works for both. Football all break is good for Ava but bad for Leo. Nobody playing is bad for both. Tag first, then football, gives them both enough. That is a compromise. If someone hits, gets hurt, or you are stuck, get a grown-up."],"adult":["This topic sits in DfE Relationships Education (2019), 'Caring friendships': most friendships have ups and downs that can be worked through, violence is never right, and pupils learn to manage conflict and seek help when needed. The PSHE Association Programme of Study covers it at Key Stage 2 under Relationships (friendships and safe relationships). In CASEL's framework it is the relationship skill 'resolving conflicts constructively'.","The lab follows a common school conflict-resolution routine. First calm down, because a child flooded with anger cannot problem-solve. Then use an I-statement (feeling plus reason, no blame), and active or reflective listening (letting the other child finish, then saying back their reason). Last, negotiate a compromise in which each side gives a little so both get enough. That is a 'win-win' rather than 'win-lose' or 'lose-lose'.","Common mistakes: trying to sort it out while still shouting; 'you' statements that blame ('You always ruin it'); planning a reply instead of listening; thinking the fair fix is whoever asked first or shouts loudest; and the quiet child who always gives in to keep the peace. That is not a fair fix, and it is worth gently noticing. Be careful, too, that children know some things are not theirs to sort out: hitting, someone being hurt or scared, or being stuck after trying. Those need a trusted adult.","In the lab your child brings Ava's cross-o-meter down with calm-down moves, picks the I-statement, picks the reply that says back Leo's reason, drags fixes onto a table and watches both friends' star meters, and sorts fall-outs friends can fix from ones that need a grown-up."]},"familyTip":"Next time siblings or friends fall out, try the four steps out loud: \"Calm first. Now each of you say I feel ... because ... . Say back what you heard. What fix gives you both enough?\" Praise the fix they find, not just the quiet.","keyIdea":"Friends fall out sometimes, and you can sort most of it out yourselves in four steps. Calm down first. Say how you feel and why, without blaming: \"I feel cross because I wanted a turn.\" Listen to your friend's side to the end, then say back what you heard. Then find a fix that gives you both enough, such as taking turns. If someone is hitting or hurt, or you are stuck, get a grown-up.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does it help sort out a fall-out? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Helps sort it out","Makes it worse"],"items":[["Take slow breaths first",0],["Shout until they give in",1],["I feel sad because I was left out",0],["You always ruin it!",1],["Take turns",0],["Walk off for ever",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagagree","caption":"Flagship lab: calm the cross-o-meter, say an I-statement, listen and say back, find a fix that fills both star meters, and know when to get a grown-up.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_33zncDHC3N":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which comment is about the work, not the person?","choices":["You're really clever.","Your diagram labels are easy to read.","You never listen.","You're bad at this."],"answer":1,"hint":"Look for the one that names part of what was made.","explain":"It names the diagram labels, a part of the work. The others judge the person."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Why is 'Make it better' not helpful feedback?","choices":["It is too unkind.","It is too long.","It does not say what to change or which part.","It uses a star."],"answer":2,"hint":"Could you act on it right now?","explain":"It is vague. Without a part and a next step, the person cannot use it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Your task is a letter asking the head teacher for a school garden. A friend says, 'Turn it into a comic.' What can you do?","choices":["You must do it, or you are being rude.","Thank them and set it aside, because a comic does not fit the task.","Tell them their idea is stupid.","Start the whole letter again."],"answer":1,"hint":"Check the tip against your task.","explain":"You decide which feedback to use. It does not fit a letter, so a polite thanks and setting it aside is fine."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which is a good star for a story opening?","choices":["It's great!","The short sentence 'Ava ran.' makes it feel fast.","You're the best writer.","Add more words."],"answer":1,"hint":"A star names one exact thing that worked.","explain":"It names the short sentence and what it does for the reader. 'Add more words' is a step, not a star."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"You get feedback and feel cross. What should you do first?","choices":["Explain why they are wrong straight away.","Listen to the end, then take a breath.","Walk off.","Give them worse feedback back."],"answer":1,"hint":"You can only decide about feedback you have heard.","explain":"Listen first. Feeling cross is normal, but defending or hitting back stops you hearing a tip that might help."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which is the best step to give on a poster about saving water?","choices":["It needs more stuff.","The writing is messy, as usual.","Add one fact with a number, like how much water a shower uses.","Cool poster."],"answer":2,"hint":"A step says one small, clear thing to try next.","explain":"It is specific and doable. 'More stuff' is vague, 'as usual' is about the person, and 'cool' is not a step."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"You get {a} tips. You use {b} of them and ask about 1. You set the rest aside. How many do you set aside?","answer":"a-b-1","hint":"Take away the ones you used and the one you asked about.","explain":"{a} take away {b} used and 1 asked about leaves {=a-b-1} set aside. Setting a tip aside is allowed if it does not fit.","params":{"a":[6,9],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"A class of {n} pupils works in pairs. Each pupil gives their partner one star and one step. How many comments are given in total?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Each pupil gives 2 comments.","explain":"{n} pupils times 2 comments each (a star and a step) is {=2*n} comments.","params":{"n":[20,30]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Someone says, 'Your talk was too fast.' Which reply helps you most?","choices":["No it wasn't.","Thanks. Was it the whole talk, or one part?","I was nervous, so it doesn't count.","OK, I'm terrible at talks."],"answer":1,"hint":"Pick the reply that helps you find what to change.","explain":"Thanking and asking which part tells you exactly what to slow down. Denying, excusing or giving up all stop you improving."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which comment is the most helpful feedback on a poem?","choices":["I like it.","Your rhyme of 'light' and 'night' sounds great. Try adding a colour word in line 3.","You're a good poet.","It's a bit boring."],"answer":1,"hint":"Look for the one that names a part of the poem and gives a next step.","explain":"It gives a star (the light and night rhyme) and a step (a colour word in line 3). The others do not say which part."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Your group gives {a} comments on your poster. {b} of them are vague, like 'nice'. How many comments are left that might be specific enough to use?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Vague comments cannot be used. Take them away from the total.","explain":"{a} take away {b} leaves {=a-b} comments that might name a part of the work you can use.","params":{"a":[6,10],"b":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Mia says, 'Your ending confused me.' What is the best first reply?","choices":["It's not confusing, you just didn't read it properly.","Thanks. Which part lost you?","Fine, I'll throw it away.","Well, yours is worse."],"answer":1,"hint":"Pick the reply that helps you find out what to fix.","explain":"Thanking and asking a question finds the exact part to fix. Blaming, giving up or hitting back all stop you learning."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"In peer review, each person gives a star and a step on {n} classmates' stories. How many stars and steps does each person give in total?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Every story gets one star and one step: two comments each.","explain":"{n} stories times 2 comments (one star, one step) is {=2*n} comments.","params":{"n":[2,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Feedback is what someone says about your work so you can make it better. It can help you a lot, or not at all. It depends on how it is said. Helpful feedback is specific. It names a part of the work. 'Your first line made me want to read on' tells the writer what to keep. 'It's good' is kind, but the writer cannot use it, because it does not say which part.","Good feedback is about the work, not the person. 'You're bad at spelling' hurts and gives no way to improve. 'Check how you spelled because in line 2' points to one thing to fix.","A simple recipe is a star and a step. The star is one thing that worked. The step is one thing to try next. Say both, and keep the step small enough to do.","When you get feedback, it can feel like someone is judging you. Try this order: listen to the end, ask if you are not sure, say thanks, then decide. You do not have to use every idea. If a tip does not fit your task, you can thank the person and set it aside."],"adult":["This lesson teaches two linked skills from the PSHE relationships strand and from classroom critique routines: giving feedback that is kind, specific and helpful, and receiving it without getting defensive. A star and a step (one thing that worked, one thing to try next) gives children a structure they can reuse in peer review, group work and home projects.","The most common slip when children give feedback is vagueness: 'nice', 'good job', 'make it better'. These feel safe but carry no information. A second mistake is confusing honesty with bluntness, as if being hurtful is the same as being helpful. Feedback that names the person ('you always', 'you're so') rather than the work is felt as an attack, so it is rarely used.","When receiving feedback, the typical reactions are to defend, make an excuse, blame the reader, or decide the whole piece is bad. Each one blocks learning. Model a calm script: 'Thanks. Which part lost you?' Asking a clarifying question is a sign of strength, not weakness.","A common misconception is that you must accept every suggestion. Children are allowed to judge feedback against their task: a tip to turn a persuasive letter into a comic does not fit, so they can thank the person and set it aside. Be careful to praise good choices about which advice to use, not only obedience. At home, give a star and a step on something low-stakes, such as a drawing or a Lego build, and ask your child to do the same for you. Then show how you respond when you get a step you did not expect."]},"familyTip":"Swap a star and a step at dinner about something each person made today: a drawing, a meal, a game level. Keep the step to one small thing. Then each person says how they will use it, or why they are setting it aside.","keyIdea":"Helpful feedback is about the work, not the person, and it is specific: it names one thing that worked (a star) and one thing to try next (a step). Vague comments like 'it's good' give nothing to use, and hurtful ones attack the person. When you receive feedback, listen first, ask a question if you are not sure what was meant, thank the person, and then decide: use it, ask about it, or set it aside if it does not fit your task.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which kind of feedback is it? Drag each comment to a box, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Vague","About the person","Helpful"],"items":[["Nice work.",0],["You're so lazy.",1],["Your graph needs a title so I know what it shows.",2],["Fix it.",0],["You always mess up.",1],["The word 'creaked' let me hear the door.",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flaggivefb","caption":"Flagship lab: sort comments, build a star and a step, choose how to respond, and decide which tips fit your task.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_z98J_Zg2L3":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What does reflecting on a friendship mean?","choices":["Looking at your own part in it, and what you could change","Working out who is to blame","Deciding you are a bad friend"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which part of a friendship can you change?","explain":"Reflecting looks at your own part, because that is the part you can change. It is not about blame."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Leo talks about himself a lot. Which is the right amount of sharing?","choices":["Sharing his news and asking about theirs","Never sharing anything","Only talking about himself"],"answer":0,"hint":"Too little and too much both cause problems.","explain":"Sharing and asking back is the middle. Never sharing makes him hard to know, and only talking about himself pushes friends away."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which is a fair strength to name?","choices":["I say sorry when I get it wrong","I am the best friend ever","I am never good at anything"],"answer":0,"hint":"A strength is something real you do.","explain":"Saying sorry is a real action you can keep doing. A boast or a put-down is not honest reflection."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Sam notices he takes over in group work. What is a goal that keeps his strength?","choices":["In group work, I will ask for two ideas before I share mine","Never give ideas again","Be less bossy"],"answer":0,"hint":"Keep the strength and change the amount.","explain":"Asking first still lets Sam lead. Never giving ideas throws a strength away, and be less bossy is too vague."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A friend says you never listen. What is a good first step?","choices":["Ask for an example and think if it is a pattern","Say she is wrong","Stop being her friend"],"answer":0,"hint":"Check what you do before you decide.","explain":"An example helps you check for a pattern. Arguing or walking away stops you learning about your own part."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Why is “I am bossy” less useful than “I often choose the game without asking”?","choices":["It labels you instead of naming an action you can change","It is too long","It is about games"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one tells you what to change?","explain":"A label sounds fixed. Naming the action shows exactly what to do differently."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Jo kept her goal on {a} of the 5 school days. On how many days did she miss it?","answer":"5-a","hint":"Take the days she kept it away from 5.","explain":"5 days take away {a} days kept leaves {=5-a} days missed. A checkable goal lets her count this.","params":{"a":[1,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"Mia tallied her week. She went along with others {a} times, which was {b} times more than she said what she wanted. How many times did she say what she wanted?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Take the difference away from the bigger number.","explain":"{a} take away {b} is {=a-b}, so she said what she wanted {=a-b} times. Going along is her pattern.","params":{"a":[6,9],"b":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Which goal can you check tomorrow?","choices":["At break, I will invite one person who is alone to join","Be nice to everyone","Try harder with friends"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does it say when, and what you will do?","explain":"It says when (at break) and what (invite one person), so you can tell if you did it."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which of these is a pattern?","choices":["Ella forgets her friend's turn in most games","Ella forgot her friend's turn once","Ella is a bad friend"],"answer":0,"hint":"A pattern happens again and again. Does it name an action?","explain":"Most games means again and again, and it names an action Ella can change. Once is a moment, and a bad friend is a label."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Over a week, Kai decided for the group {a} times and asked others {b} times. How many moments did he look back on?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Add the two kinds of moment together.","explain":"{a} times deciding plus {b} times asking makes {=a+b} moments to look back on.","params":{"a":[3,6],"b":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Ava always stays quiet when she disagrees with friends. What is the best goal?","choices":["When I disagree with a plan, I will say once, kindly, what I would like","Always argue back","Be braver"],"answer":0,"hint":"Pick a goal that says when and what, and does not swing too far.","explain":"It says when (I disagree with a plan) and what (say it once, kindly). Always argue back goes too far, and be braver is too vague to check."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Priya writes {a} notes about herself. {b} are strengths. How many are stretches?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Take the strengths away from all the notes.","explain":"{a} notes take away {b} strengths leaves {=a-b} stretches to work on.","params":{"a":[6,9],"b":[2,5]}}],"explain":{"kid":["When a friendship goes wrong, it is easy to look only at the other person. Reflecting means asking: what is my part? Not to blame yourself, but because your part is the bit you can change.","Start with patterns. One time is just a moment. A pattern is something you do again and again. If you picked the game on Monday, Wednesday and Friday, that is a pattern. Thinking back over a week helps you see it.","Most habits are not simply good or bad. Taking the lead gets a group started. Too much, and others stop sharing ideas. Letting small things go keeps the peace. Always staying quiet means friends never know what you want.","Be fair to yourself. Name a strength, like keeping secrets or saying sorry, and one stretch to work on. Then make a goal you can check: At break, I will ask two friends what they want to play before I suggest a game."],"adult":["This topic is part of self-awareness and relationship skills in the CASEL framework. The PSHE Association programme for Key Stage 2 asks pupils to say what makes a good friendship. It also asks them to think about their own behaviour in it. At 9 to 11, children can start to see their own habits from the outside.","Three ideas carry the topic. A pattern is a habit seen across many moments, so one incident is weak evidence. Most social habits, like leading, going along and talking about yourself, help in the right amount and cause harm at either extreme. A useful goal is specific and easy to check. Researchers call this an implementation intention: when this happens, I will do that.","Common mistakes: treating one incident as a pattern; labelling the whole self (I am bossy) instead of naming an action; listing only faults, which lowers mood but rarely changes much; swinging to the other extreme (never lead again); blaming the other person; and setting vague goals such as be nicer.","In the lab, learners tally six moments from one week until a pattern shows. They slide three habits to find the amount that works for friends. They sort Priya's notes into strengths and stretches, and choose goals they could check tomorrow."]},"familyTip":"At dinner, each person shares one thing they did well with a friend this week and one thing they might do differently. Help your child turn the second one into a when, I will goal, and ask how it went a few days later.","keyIdea":"Reflecting on a friendship means looking at your own part, not only at what others do. Look for patterns: things you do again and again, like always choosing the game or staying quiet when you disagree. Most habits help in the right amount and cause problems when there is too much or too little. Name what you do well as well as what to work on. Then turn one pattern into a goal you can check: when, and what you will do.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is each goal one you could check tomorrow, or too vague? Drag each goal to a box, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["You can check it","Too vague"],"items":[["At lunch, I will ask one friend how their day was",0],["Be kinder",1],["When I disagree, I will say so once, calmly",0],["Be a better friend",1],["In group work, I will let two people speak before me",0],["Stop being annoying",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagmirror","caption":"Flagship lab: tally your moments to find a pattern, slide habits to the right amount, sort strengths and stretches, and choose a goal you can check.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_YFDV-uG4rX":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is coercive control?","choices":["A pattern of control, threats or put-downs over time","One argument","Only hitting someone"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what keeps happening, not one day.","explain":"Coercive control is a repeated pattern. It can be emotional or digital, not just physical, and it is a crime."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Someone says no. What is the respectful thing to do?","choices":["Stop, and accept it","Keep asking until they agree","Act hurt so they change their mind"],"answer":0,"hint":"Consent is given freely. Can it be worn down?","explain":"Accept the no. Pushing or guilt-tripping is pressure, and consent given under pressure is not freely given."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Sam asks for your password to prove you trust them. Which reply is assertive?","choices":["\"I keep my passwords private. That is not about trust.\"","Give it so Sam is not upset","Shout and walk out"],"answer":0,"hint":"Assertive means clear, calm and firm.","explain":"A calm, clear no sets the boundary. Giving in teaches them your no can be pushed; shouting can make things unsafe."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A friend says their partner scares them. Best first step?","choices":["Listen, then tell a trusted adult","Confront the partner yourself","Promise to keep it secret"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which keeps everyone safest?","explain":"Listen without blame and get a trusted adult involved. Confronting the partner or keeping it secret can increase the risk."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Where does 'threatens to share your photos if you end it' sit?","choices":["Abusive","Healthy","Unhealthy but fine"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is this a threat that uses fear to control you?","explain":"It is abusive and it is a crime. Sharing or threatening to share intimate images is against the law."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"They are kind most days but cut you off from friends. Is it healthy?","choices":["No, the pattern still matters","Yes, the kind days cancel it","Yes, if they say sorry"],"answer":0,"hint":"Kind days can sit inside a pattern. What keeps happening?","explain":"Kind days and apologies often sit inside controlling patterns. The repeated isolation is the warning sign."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A partner sent {p} angry texts an hour for {h} hours because Jay did not reply. How many texts?","answer":"p*h","hint":"Multiply the texts each hour by the hours.","explain":"{p} times {h} is {=p*h} texts. Demanding instant replies is monitoring, a warning sign of control.","params":{"p":[2,6],"h":[2,3]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"Jay noted {a} controlling moments on Monday and {b} on Tuesday. How many in total?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Add the two days together to see the pattern.","explain":"{a} plus {b} is {=a+b}. Writing moments down helps show a pattern to a trusted adult.","params":{"a":[1,4],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Which number is Childline in the UK?","choices":["0800 1111","999","111"],"answer":0,"hint":"999 is for danger right now. Which is the free helpline for young people?","explain":"Childline is 0800 1111, free and confidential. Call 999 (or 911 in the US) if someone is in danger now."},{"type":"mc","id":"m10","prompt":"Your partner wants your location shared all the time. What is it?","choices":["A warning sign of control","Just being caring","Normal if you are close"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is it checking you got home, or watching you all the time?","explain":"Constant tracking is monitoring, a warning sign. Caring is checking you got home safe and letting it go."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which is a sign of a healthy relationship?","choices":["You can say no and it is respected","They decide who you can see","They need your passwords"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one leaves you free to make your own choices?","explain":"Respect means your no counts. Choosing who you see or taking your passwords is control."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Jay's partner checked Jay's phone {n} times a week for {w} weeks. How many checks is that in all?","answer":"n*w","hint":"Multiply the checks each week by the number of weeks.","explain":"{n} times {w} is {=n*w} checks. When it keeps happening, it is a pattern of control, not a one-off.","params":{"n":[2,5],"w":[2,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Jealousy that stops you seeing friends shows they really care. True or false?","choices":["False, it is a warning sign","True, it shows love","True, if they say sorry"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does it give you more freedom, or less?","explain":"Caring gives you space. Jealousy that cuts you off from friends is a warning sign of control."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"In a week, Mo had {c} days with put-downs or tracking. How many of the 7 days were free of them?","answer":"7-c","hint":"Take the control days away from the 7 days.","explain":"7 take away {c} is {=7-c}. Kind days do not cancel the {c} control days. The pattern still matters.","params":{"c":[1,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A healthy relationship feels safe. You can be yourself. You can see your friends. When you say no, the other person listens and stops. These are sometimes called green flags.","Some signs are red flags. A partner who reads your messages or gets angry when you see friends is trying to control you. Jealousy like that is not proof of love. It is a warning sign.","Relationships sit on a line: healthy, then unhealthy, then abuse, like threats. Control is often a pattern, not one bad day. When put-downs and tracking happen again and again, it is called coercive control. It is against the law.","A boundary is what you will and will not do. Say it clearly and calmly, then stick to it. If you or a friend are not safe, tell a trusted adult or call Childline on 0800 1111. In danger now, call 999 or 911."],"adult":["This lesson follows the DfE statutory guidance for Relationships and Sex Education in secondary schools in England. It covers what makes a relationship healthy: trust, respect, honesty, kindness, boundaries and consent. It also covers how to spot unhealthy and abusive behaviour. Coercive control has been a crime in England and Wales since 2015. The PSHE Association plan for ages 14 to 16 covers the same ground.","The lab shows relationships on a line, not as good or bad. Sulking and guilt-trips are unhealthy. When they turn into a pattern of tracking, cutting someone off from friends, put-downs and threats, it is abuse. Students learn to look at what keeps happening over time. One bad day, or one kind gesture, tells you very little.","A common mistake is to think jealousy shows love. Many teens say constant checking is a sign of caring. Another is to think abuse must be physical, so control by phone or by words is missed. Students also often think kind days cancel out the bad ones. That is how control keeps going.","The boundaries step shows assertive replies: short, calm and clear about what the student will or will not do. Be careful not to mix up assertive with aggressive. On getting help, students practise listening without blame and telling a trusted adult. Confronting a partner, or promising to keep it secret, can make things more dangerous."]},"keyIdea":"A healthy relationship rests on respect, trust, honesty and the freedom to say no. Relationships sit on a spectrum from healthy to unhealthy to abusive. Warning signs include jealousy that stops you seeing friends, checking your phone, put-downs and pressure after a no. Coercive control is a pattern of these over time, and it is a crime. Setting a clear, calm boundary is assertive. If you or a friend are not safe, tell a trusted adult or call Childline on 0800 1111.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Green flag or red flag? Drag each one, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Healthy (green flag)","Warning sign (red flag)"],"items":[["Listens when you say no",0],["Checks your phone",1],["Happy you have other friends",0],["Puts you down in front of others",1],["Trusts you to go out",0],["Demands your passwords",1],["Says sorry and changes",0],["Threatens to leave if you see friends",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flaghealthy","caption":"Flagship lab: sort red and green flags, use the healthy–abusive spectrum, spot control across a week and set a boundary.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_nqM2OW0Qlm":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is a social cue?","choices":["A message sent without words, like a look or where someone sits","A rule written down by the school","A message in a group chat only"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of what people show rather than say.","explain":"Social cues are signals such as facial expressions, body language and seating. They are clues, not proof."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"In Tajfel's experiments, teams were based on a tiny, random difference. What happened?","choices":["Most people still favoured their own team","People shared everything equally","People favoured the other team"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does a group need a good reason to feel like 'us'?","explain":"Even meaningless groups made people favour 'us'. That pull helps explain why groups can leave others out."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"What usually happens to a story as it is passed from person to person?","choices":["Details drop out and the drama grows","It stays exactly the same","It becomes more accurate"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the gossip chain in the lab.","explain":"Retold stories lose details and sharpen the dramatic parts, so rumours become less true and more hurtful."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Someone says, 'Did you hear about Jo?' What stops the rumour?","choices":["\"I don't know if that's true. I'd rather not pass it on.\"","Listening, then telling one other person","Asking everyone if it is true"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which choice means the story goes no further?","explain":"Refusing to pass it on stops the chain with you. Asking around or telling 'just one person' still spreads it."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"A rumour starts with {s} people. Each one tells {k} others. How many new people hear it in that round?","answer":"s*k","hint":"Each of the {s} people tells {k} others.","explain":"{s} × {k} = {=s*k} new people, from just one round of retelling.","params":{"s":[2,4],"k":[2,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which reply is passive?","choices":["\"It's fine…\" when it really is not","\"I'd like a turn too, please.\"","\"Give it here or else.\""],"answer":0,"hint":"Passive hides what you really feel or need.","explain":"Saying 'it's fine' when it isn't hides your feelings, so nothing changes. The second is assertive and the third aggressive."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"You see photos of a hangout you weren't invited to. Which response is assertive?","choices":["Message a friend privately: 'Felt a bit left out. Can I come next time?'","Post an angry comment under the photos","Leave every group chat without saying why"],"answer":0,"hint":"Calm, honest and private.","explain":"Saying how you felt and asking for what you want is assertive. Public anger is aggressive; silently leaving is passive."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"In a points game with {t} points, you give your own team {b} and the other team the rest. How many more points does your team get than the other team?","answer":"2*b-t","hint":"Work out the other team's points first, then find the difference.","explain":"The other team gets {t} - {b} = {=t-b}, so the gap is {b} - {=t-b} = {=2*b-t}. Tajfel found many people chose to make the gap big, even at a cost.","params":{"t":[10,14],"b":[8,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Your group starts mocking a new student. What keeps both your values and your calm?","choices":["A short 'Not funny' and changing the subject","Laughing along to fit in","Shouting that they are all cruel"],"answer":0,"hint":"Calm and short is easier for the group to accept.","explain":"A brief, calm comment protects the student without a fight. Laughing along drops your values; shouting makes the group defend the joke."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Your friend does not reply to your message all evening. What is the best first step?","choices":["Wait, then ask kindly tomorrow if everything is OK","Decide they are angry with you","Tell the group they are ignoring you"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is one missing reply proof of anything?","explain":"A missing reply is an unclear cue. They might be busy or have no phone. Checking kindly beats assuming the worst."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"In a points game you share {t} points between your team and the other team. You give your own team {b}. How many points does the other team get?","answer":"t-b","hint":"Both teams' points add up to the total.","explain":"{t} - {b} = {=t-b}. Most people in Tajfel's experiments gave their own team the bigger share, even when teams were random.","params":{"t":[10,14],"b":[8,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which reply is assertive?","choices":["\"I didn't like that joke about me. Please stop.\"","\"Say that again and see what happens.\"","Laughing along even though it hurt"],"answer":0,"hint":"Assertive is clear about your feeling and respectful to the other person.","explain":"The first says how you feel and what you need, without attacking. The threat is aggressive; laughing along is passive."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"You tell a rumour to {k} friends. Each of them tells {k} more people. How many people have heard it from those two rounds?","answer":"k+k*k","hint":"Count the first round, then each of those people tells {k} more.","explain":"{k} in the first round plus {k} × {k} = {=k*k} in the second makes {=k+k*k}. That is why stopping it at the start matters most.","params":{"k":[2,5]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Groups send messages without words. Someone turns away, laughs at a joke you missed or moves a bag onto a seat. These are social cues. They are clues, not proof. If a cue is unclear, check it. Ask 'Is this seat free?' before you decide that people do not want you there.","Groups also change how we think. In a famous set of experiments, Henri Tajfel split teenagers into teams with tiny, random differences. Most still gave their own team more points than the other team. Just being an 'us' makes people favour their group. That same pull can make a group leave someone out.","Gossip is talk about people who are not there. It can make a group feel close, but every retelling changes the story. Details drop out and the drama grows. By the fifth person, 'Maya missed the party' can become 'Maya thinks she is too good for us.' The easiest place to stop a rumour is with you.","When something bothers you, there are three ways to respond. Passive means staying quiet or saying 'it's fine' when it is not. Aggressive means attacking the person. Assertive means saying how you feel and what you need, calmly and with respect. It is not about volume. A short, calm 'Not funny' can protect someone and keep your values."],"adult":["The DfE guidance on Relationships, Sex and Health Education (2019) says secondary pupils should learn about respectful friendships, and how to manage conflict and peer pressure. The PSHE Association's Key Stage 3 plan covers peer influence, being assertive, gossip and how to deal with being left out.","The most common mistake at this age is to read one unclear cue as proof that others reject you. Psychologists call this a hostile attribution bias, and it leads to more fights. Children also think passing on gossip word for word does no harm, mix up assertive with loud or rude, and think groups only form for good reasons.","The lab draws on two classic studies. In Tajfel's 'minimal group' tests, even random teams made people favour their own side. In Allport and Postman's rumour studies, each retelling lost detail and made the drama bigger. Useful words are social cue, in-group and out-group, rumour, passive, assertive and aggressive.","The lab has five short scenes. Your child reads mixed cues at a lunch table and splits points between a coin-toss team and another. They then stop a rumour before it turns cruel, build an assertive reply on a gauge and choose a path through being left out."]},"familyTip":"When your child describes a confusing moment with friends, ask 'What did you actually see or hear?' and 'What else could it mean?' before talking about what to do. Then practise one calm, assertive sentence together.","keyIdea":"Groups talk in cues as well as words, but a cue is a clue, not proof, so check before you assume the worst. Being in a group, even one picked by a coin toss, pulls us to favour 'us' over 'them', and gossip spreads that pull while getting less true each time it is retold. Being assertive means saying what you need clearly and respectfully, so you can belong without giving up your values.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Passive, assertive or aggressive? Drag each reply, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Passive","Assertive","Aggressive"],"items":[["\"It's fine, whatever.\" (it isn't)",0],["\"I felt left out. Can I come next time?\"",1],["\"You're all so fake.\"",2],["Staying silent and leaving the chat",0],["\"Please ask before you post my photo.\"",1],["\"Delete it or you'll be sorry.\"",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagcues","caption":"Flagship lab: read mixed cues, feel the pull of a coin-toss team, stop a rumour, build an assertive reply and handle being left out.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_NCrbQe0LdB":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What are the four parts of a clean message, in order?","choices":["Observation, feeling, need, request","Blame, feeling, demand, sorry","Judgement, feeling, threat, request"],"answer":0,"hint":"Start with what happened and end with what you are asking for.","explain":"Observation (what happened), feeling, need, then request. It names the problem without attacking the person."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"\"I feel that you're lying to me.\" Is this a feeling?","choices":["No, it's a judgement dressed as a feeling","Yes, it starts with 'I feel'","Yes, because it's honest"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is 'lying' something you feel, or something you think they did?","explain":"'I feel that…' usually hides a judgement about the other person. A feeling would be 'worried' or 'hurt'."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which apology takes real responsibility?","choices":["I shared your photo without asking. That was wrong of me.","Sorry, but everyone does it.","Sorry you feel that way."],"answer":0,"hint":"Does it name what was done and own it with no excuse?","explain":"It names the action and owns it. 'But everyone does it' is an excuse, and 'sorry you feel that way' blames their feelings."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A friend says sorry properly, then keeps doing the same thing. What happens to trust?","choices":["It falls further, because the words weren't backed up","It goes back to full straight away","Nothing changes"],"answer":0,"hint":"Trust is rebuilt by what people do over time.","explain":"Words start the repair, but broken promises after an apology make trust fall further. Actions over weeks rebuild it."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Trust starts at {s}% and rises {k} points each week you keep your word. What is it after {w} weeks?","answer":"s+k*w","hint":"Work out {k} × {w}, then add {s}.","explain":"{s} + {k} × {w} = {=s+k*w}%. Steady effort, week by week, is what rebuilds trust.","params":{"s":[10,30],"k":[5,10],"w":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Why is avoiding a problem with a friend often worse in the long run?","choices":["The hurt stays and builds up, and comes out in small digs","Problems always disappear on their own","Avoiding it makes the friendship stronger"],"answer":0,"hint":"What happened to the 'avoid it' line in the lab?","explain":"Avoided hurt doesn't vanish. It builds up and slowly cools the friendship, while an honest talk can repair it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which opening gives your friend the best chance to listen?","choices":["When you read my messages without asking…","You're so nosy, you always do this…","Everyone says you can't be trusted…"],"answer":0,"hint":"Pick the one that describes what happened.","explain":"Starting with the action they took lets them hear it. Labels, 'always' and 'everyone says' make people defend themselves."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"What should a real apology say about the future?","choices":["What you will do differently, so they can check","Nothing: the past is the past","That it will never be mentioned again"],"answer":0,"hint":"How can the other person know things will change?","explain":"Saying what will change (and then doing it) is how an apology turns into repair."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"Your friend sent {m} messages and you replied to {r} of them. How many went unanswered?","answer":"m-r","hint":"Take the replies away from all the messages.","explain":"{m} − {r} = {=m-r} unanswered. 'You didn't reply to {=m-r} of my messages' is an observation, not a judgement.","params":{"m":[6,10],"r":[1,4]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which of these is an observation, not a judgement?","choices":["You left before the end of my match.","You never support me.","You're a fake friend."],"answer":0,"hint":"Could a camera record it? Does it use always or never?","explain":"'You left before the end of my match' is a fact anyone could see. The others label the person or use 'never'."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"After an apology, trust is at {t}%. Each week you keep your word it goes up 10 points. What is it after {w} weeks?","answer":"t+10*w","hint":"Add 10 points for each of the {w} weeks to {t}.","explain":"{t} + 10 × {w} = {=t+10*w}%. Trust grows a little each week, not all at once.","params":{"t":[20,40],"w":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"\"Sorry if you were upset, but it was only a joke.\" What makes this a face-saving sorry?","choices":["It doubts their feelings and shrinks what happened","It is too short","It uses the word sorry"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at 'if', 'but' and 'only a joke'.","explain":"'If' doubts that they were hurt, and 'but… only a joke' shrinks it and shifts the blame. It does not name or own what was done."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Trust is back up to {t}%. Then you break your promise and it drops by 25 points. What is it now?","answer":"t-25","hint":"Take 25 points away from {t}.","explain":"{t} − 25 = {=t-25}%. One slip can undo two or three weeks of kept promises.","params":{"t":[40,90]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which is a clear request?","choices":["Could you tell me if plans change, so I'm not waiting?","Just stop being like this.","You need to change."],"answer":0,"hint":"Does it ask for one action they could say yes to?","explain":"It asks for one specific action. 'Stop being like this' and 'you need to change' are vague and sound like orders."}],"explain":{"kid":["When a friend hurts you, it is tempting to say 'You're so selfish' or 'You always do this.' Those are judgements: they label the person, so your friend defends themselves instead of listening. Start with an observation instead: what actually happened, the kind of thing a camera could record. 'You told Mia what I said about the party.'","Then say how you felt, what you need and what you are asking for. 'I felt embarrassed and hurt, because I need to trust that private things stay private. Could you check with me before you share something I told you?' Watch out for 'I feel that you are a liar.' That is a judgement in disguise. Real feelings are words like hurt, worried or embarrassed.","If you are the one who caused the hurt, a real apology does four things. It names what you did, owns it with no excuses, shows you see how it hurt them, and says what will change. A face-saving sorry just ends the awkward moment: 'Sorry if you were upset', 'I was only joking', or 'Sorry, but you started it.' The word 'but' cancels everything before it.","Saying sorry starts the repair, but trust comes back slowly, through what you do week after week. One slip can undo weeks of effort. Avoiding the problem feels easier, but the hurt stays and comes out later. Friends who talk it through honestly often end up closer than before."],"adult":["Ages 12 to 13. In England, the 2019 guidance for Relationships Education says secondary pupils should learn what makes a healthy friendship, including trust. They should also learn practical ways to manage conflict and repair a friendship. The PSHE Association's KS3 plan covers conflict, saying sorry and forgiving. Key words: observation, judgement, feeling, need, request, responsibility, apology, trust, repair.","The four-part message comes from Marshall Rosenberg's Nonviolent Communication: observation, feeling, need, request. Studies of teenage friendships suggest that how a conflict is handled matters more than whether it happens. Talking it out and making up, rather than avoiding it or forcing a win, goes with friendships that last (see Laursen and colleagues on peer conflict).","Common mistakes: using 'always' or 'never', which turns a fact into a judgement; 'I feel that you…', which is a judgement dressed as a feeling; vague requests ('be better') or orders; the 'sorry if you were upset' apology; 'but' and 'only joking', which shift the blame or shrink the hurt; and expecting trust to come back the moment you say sorry. Long excuses also sound like defending, so keep an apology short.","In the lab your child sorts six things people say into facts and judgements. They build a four-part message until each part is clean, and build an apology while a repair meter reacts to blame and 'if'. Then they rebuild trust over eight weeks and compare ten weeks of avoiding a problem with talking it through. This is about everyday falling-outs. Bullying, threats or abuse need an adult's help, not just a good talk."]},"familyTip":"Next time there is a falling-out at home, try the four parts out loud yourself: 'When the plates were left in the sink (what happened), I felt fed up (feeling), because I need help with the washing up (need). Could you clear them tonight (request)?' Then ask your child to spot the four parts. If you owe an apology, model one with no 'but'.","keyIdea":"An honest conversation goes after the problem, not the person. Start with what actually happened (an observation), say how you felt, say what you need, and ask for one clear thing (a request). If you are the one who did the harm, a real apology names exactly what you did, takes responsibility with no 'but', shows you understand the hurt, and says what will change. Saying sorry starts the repair; trust comes back slowly, through weeks of keeping your word. Friendships that get through a conflict honestly often end up stronger than ones that avoid it.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"A real apology or a face-saving sorry? Drag each card to a box, or tap the card then a box.","opts":{"bins":["Real apology","Face-saving sorry"],"items":[["I'm sorry I read your messages. That was wrong.",0],["Sorry if you were upset.",1],["I see it embarrassed you. I'll ask first from now on.",0],["Sorry, but you overreact to everything.",1],["I was only joking, chill.",1],["It was my choice, and I'm sorry.",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagrepair","caption":"Flagship lab: sort facts from judgements, build a clean four-part message, build a real apology, rebuild trust week by week, and compare avoiding with talking.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_X7Tu94-a2m":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Sam moved to another class, and now you play together less. Whose fault is it?","choices":["Nobody's: things changed","Yours","Sam's"],"answer":0,"hint":"What changed: the class, or how kind you both are?","explain":"Sam moving class means you see each other less. Drifting apart like this is usually nobody's fault."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which thought helps when a friend drifts away?","choices":["I feel sad now. I can make new friends too.","Nobody will ever like me.","It is all my fault."],"answer":0,"hint":"Which thought is kind to you and helps you try again?","explain":"It is OK to feel sad, and it helps to remember you can make new friends. The other two thoughts are not true and make you feel worse."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Priya moved to a new school. How can you stay friends?","choices":["Make her a card or call with a grown-up's help","Forget her straight away","Be cross with her for moving"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one shows Priya you still care?","explain":"A card or a call tells Priya you still care. She did nothing wrong by moving."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"At the start of the year you had {a} close friends. {b} of them moved class. How many close friends are still in your class?","answer":"a-b","where":"b<a","hint":"Take away the friends who moved class.","explain":"{a} take away {b} is {=a-b}. The friends who moved can still be your friends at playtime.","params":{"a":[3,6],"b":[1,2]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"How many friends can you have at once?","choices":["Lots, old and new","Only one best friend","None, if one friend leaves"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can your friend have other friends too?","explain":"You can have lots of friends at the same time. New friends do not push out old ones."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Zara's group laughs at you every day. What should you do?","choices":["Tell a grown-up you trust","Say it is a normal change","Keep it to yourself"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is laughing at someone every day kind?","explain":"Laughing at someone every day is unkind, not a normal change. A trusted grown-up can help stop it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Your friend wants to play with someone new today. What could you say?","choices":["'Can I play too?'","'Choose: me or them!'","'I am never playing with you again.'"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which answer keeps the friendship and lets everyone play?","explain":"Asking to join keeps your friendship and makes a new one. Friends should not have to choose."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"Jo goes to art club {n} times a week and you go too. How many times do you see Jo at art club in 2 weeks?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Count the times in one week, then double it for 2 weeks.","explain":"{n} times a week for 2 weeks is {=2*n} times. Spending time together on things you both like is how new friends grow close.","params":{"n":[1,4]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Your best friend joins a club and plays with new children. What does this most likely mean?","choices":["Friendships change, and that is normal","Your friend hates you now","Something is wrong with you"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what changed: the club, not you.","explain":"A new club means new people to play with. Friendships change like this all the time, and nobody did anything wrong."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"You have {a} friends in your class. You join a club and make {b} new friends. How many friends do you have now?","answer":"a+b","hint":"New friends add to the friends you already have. Add them together.","explain":"{a} friends and {b} new ones makes {=a+b}. Making new friends does not mean losing the old ones.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which is a good way to make a new friend?","choices":["Ask, 'Can I play too?'","Wait alone until someone asks you","Give them sweets so they like you"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one is a small, friendly step you can take yourself?","explain":"Asking to join a game is a quick, friendly way to start. Waiting can take a long time, and sweets do not make a friend."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Last year {a} children were in your friendship ring. {b} moved away, and you made {c} new friends. How many are in your ring now?","answer":"a-b+c","where":"b<a","hint":"Take away the friends who moved away, then add the new ones.","explain":"{a} take away {b} is {=a-b}, and {c} new friends makes {=a-b+c}. Rings change, and they can grow again.","params":{"a":[3,7],"b":[1,2],"c":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Someone tells everyone not to play with you, every day. What is this?","choices":["Unkind, so tell a trusted grown-up","A normal friendship change","Something to keep secret"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is this people's likes changing, or someone leaving you out on purpose?","explain":"Leaving someone out on purpose, again and again, is unkind and can be bullying. A trusted grown-up can help."}],"explain":{"kid":["Friends do not always stay the same. A friend may move class, join a new club or start to like new games. Then you may see them less, and you can drift apart. This happens to everyone.","When a friend drifts away, you might feel sad or left out. That is OK. It does not mean something is wrong with you. It is usually nobody's fault, and the sad feeling gets smaller.","You can make new friends. Join a club you like. Ask, 'Can I play too?' Invite someone to play, or ask what they like doing. You can keep old friends too, with a wave, a card or a play date.","Some things are not normal change. If someone leaves you out on purpose, tells others not to play with you or laughs at you every day, that is unkind. Tell a grown-up you trust."],"adult":["Ages 7 to 9. This is the Caring friendships part of the DfE statutory guidance for Relationships Education in primary schools. Pupils should know how people choose and make friends. They should know that healthy friendships do not make others feel lonely or left out. And they should know that most friendships have ups and downs, which can often be worked through. Key words: friendship, drift apart, change, feelings, exclusion, unkind behaviour, bullying, trusted adult.","At this age many children have one best friend, so any change can feel like being dropped. In fact friendships in middle childhood shift often, as classes, clubs and interests change. Children who know how to join in and invite others cope better. The skills to practise are naming the feeling, keeping a kind inner voice and taking one small step.","Common mistakes: thinking a friend who plays with someone else hates you; blaming yourself or the other child when you drift apart; waiting alone for someone else to make the first move; trying to buy a friend with gifts; and making a friend choose between you and others. Be careful not to call leaving out on purpose or daily teasing a normal change. That is unkind, and if it keeps happening it is bullying, so a trusted adult needs to know.","In the lab your child slides a school year from Autumn to Summer on friendship rings and finds out why Sam, Ava and Jo moved. They sort six cases into normal change or unkind, swap three unhelpful thoughts for kinder ones, pick the four ideas that help make a new friend, and choose three ways to stay friends with Sam."]},"familyTip":"Draw your child's friendship rings together: you in the middle, close friends, friends, people you know. Talk about who has moved in or out since last year and why. Then plan one small step, like inviting someone from a club to the park, and share a time a friendship of yours changed too.","keyIdea":"Friendships change as people change class, join new clubs or find new things they like. A friend drifting away is normal and is usually nobody's fault, and it does not mean something is wrong with you. It is OK to feel sad, and the feeling gets smaller. You can keep old friends and make new ones by joining in, asking and inviting. Being left out on purpose or laughed at is not a normal change: that is unkind, and you should tell a trusted grown-up.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Normal change, or unkind? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Normal change","Unkind: tell a grown-up"],"items":[["Moved to a new school",0],["Told others not to play with you",1],["Likes a new game now",0],["Laughs at you every day",1],["Goes to a club at lunch",0],["Spreads mean stories about you",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagfchg","caption":"Flagship lab: slide a school year on friendship rings, sort change from unkindness, swap unhelpful thoughts, make and keep friends.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_8StiXnYq1u":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which leadership style is the best one?","choices":["It depends on the situation","Democratic, always","Directive, always"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of a fire drill, then of choosing a class trip.","explain":"Each style fits some moments. Good leaders switch: orders in danger, a vote for shared choices, help for someone stuck."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Two people do all the talking in a meeting. What should the leader do?","choices":["Ask quieter people by name","Let the talkers decide","Ask the room, “Any other ideas?”"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who answers an open question to the whole room?","explain":"Open questions are usually answered by the same confident people. Asking by name, or a go-round, brings in new ideas."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which line is fair persuasion?","choices":["“Here is why it matters. You can still say no.”","“Everyone else already said yes.”","“Do not tell your parents.”"],"answer":0,"hint":"Fair influence gives reasons and leaves the choice free.","explain":"Reasons plus a free choice is persuasion. A fake crowd is pressure, and secrecy from trusted adults is a warning sign."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A friend says yes only because they are scared to say no. Is that real consent?","choices":["No: consent has to be freely given","Yes: they said yes","Yes, if it is a small thing"],"answer":0,"hint":"Could they have said no with nothing bad happening?","explain":"Consent means a free choice. A yes given out of fear or pressure is not consent, whatever the size of the thing."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"What makes a leader transformational?","choices":["They share a goal that inspires people to grow","They give strict orders","They let everyone do what they like"],"answer":0,"hint":"Transform means change. What do they change in their team?","explain":"A transformational leader paints a shared goal that makes people want to do more and get better."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"A team leader threatens to tell the teacher who is lazy. Effort goes up. What usually happens next?","choices":["Trust falls, and people stop helping when not watched","Trust goes up too","Nothing changes"],"answer":0,"hint":"Do people trust someone who threatens them?","explain":"Threats can buy effort for a moment, but trust drops. People do the minimum and drift away."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"In a vote, {y} people pick the beach and {p} pick the park. How many more people chose the beach?","answer":"y-p","hint":"Find the difference between the two numbers.","explain":"{y} − {p} = {=y-p}. A democratic leader goes with the vote, and makes sure the others felt heard.","params":{"y":[12,20],"p":[3,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"A leader agrees with Ben in front of him, then changes the plan back in secret. What is this?","choices":["Manipulation","Democratic leadership","Servant leadership"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is it honest? Does Ben really get a say?","explain":"Pretending to agree and then going behind his back is manipulation. When Ben finds out, trust collapses."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"A club of {n} people does a go-round. Before it, {q} people had not spoken. How many people had spoken before the go-round?","answer":"n-q","hint":"Take the quiet people away from the whole club.","explain":"{n} − {q} = {=n-q} had spoken. The go-round gave the other {q} a turn too.","params":{"n":[8,15],"q":[2,6]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"“Fire alarm. Everyone out the side door, now.” Which style is this?","choices":["Directive","Democratic","Servant"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who decides here, and is there time to talk?","explain":"The leader gives clear, fast orders. That is directive, and it is right in an emergency."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"A teammate is stuck and stressed the night before a deadline. Which style fits best?","choices":["Servant: ask what they need and help","Directive: tell them to hurry","Democratic: let the group vote on it"],"answer":0,"hint":"What does a stuck, stressed person need most?","explain":"A servant leader removes what is blocking someone. Orders or a vote do not unstick them."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A group of {n} people meets. Only 2 of them have spoken. How many people have not been heard yet?","answer":"n-2","hint":"Take the people who have spoken from the whole group.","explain":"{n} − 2 = {=n-2} people have not spoken. A good leader asks them by name or goes round the circle.","params":{"n":[4,12]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"In a go-round, each of {n} people gets {t} minutes to share an idea. How many minutes does the go-round take?","answer":"n*t","hint":"Everyone gets the same time. Multiply.","explain":"{n} × {t} = {=n*t} minutes. Fair shares of time stop two voices taking over.","params":{"n":[4,10],"t":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"“Do it or you are out of the group chat.” What is this?","choices":["Coercion: a threat","Persuasion: a reason","Good leadership: being firm"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does it give a reason, or threaten a loss?","explain":"It threatens to exclude someone if they refuse. That is coercion. A yes given to avoid it is not a free choice."}],"explain":{"kid":["Leading is not just being the boss. There are different styles. A directive leader gives clear orders. A democratic leader lets the group decide, often by a vote. A servant leader asks what others need, then helps them. A transformational leader shares a big goal that makes people want to grow.","No style is best all the time. In a fire drill you need fast orders, not a vote. When the class picks a trip, everyone should get a say. When a friend is stuck, they need help, not a speech. Good leaders switch style to fit the moment.","In most groups a few people do most of the talking. That does not mean they have the best ideas. A good leader asks quiet people by name, or goes round the circle so everyone gets a turn.","Leaders have influence, and that comes with a duty. Fair influence gives reasons and leaves people free to say no. Threats, shaming, fake crowds and secrets are pressure. They may work for a moment, but trust falls. If someone says yes only because they are scared, that is not real consent."],"adult":["This fits the PSHE Association plan for Key Stage 3: healthy relationships, speaking up for yourself, peer influence and spotting coercion. It also fits the DfE guidance on respectful relationships and consent. The four styles are the usual school model. Directive and democratic come from studies of boys' clubs by Lewin and his team in 1939. Servant leadership is from Greenleaf (1970). Transformational leadership is from Burns (1978) and Bass (1985). The idea that the best style depends on the task comes from Hersey and Blanchard.","The lab treats including quiet people as a skill to practise. In most group talks a few people take most of the time. A question thrown to the whole room is mostly answered by the most confident. Asking by name, going round the circle, or writing ideas down first gives everyone a turn, and it brings out ideas that were there all along.","Common mistakes: thinking one style is always right; thinking a leader must be loud or bossy; calling threats or public shaming 'being firm'; missing fake crowd pressure or a request for secrecy as tricks; and thinking a yes counts as consent even when it came from fear. Say clearly that pressure from a friend or partner is never their fault, and that they can always talk to a trusted adult."]},"familyTip":"Talk about a team, club or group you have both been in. Who led it, and how? When did that style work, and when did it not? Then ask: what is the difference between persuading someone and pressuring them?","keyIdea":"There is more than one way to lead. A directive leader gives clear orders. A democratic leader lets the group decide. A servant leader asks what people need and helps. A transformational leader shares a goal that inspires. No style is always best: pick the one that fits the moment. Good leaders also make room for quiet voices, and they influence people with reasons, never with threats, tricks or pressure. A yes given out of fear is not a free choice.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which leadership style is each leader using? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["directive","democratic","servant","transformational"],"items":[["“Stop. Stand back from the road.”",0],["“Hands up for the film you want.”",1],["“How can I make your job easier?”",2],["“Let’s be the first class to win the green flag.”",3],["“Tom, fetch the first-aid kit now.”",0],["“Let’s hear both plans, then decide.”",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flaglead","caption":"Flagship lab: name four styles, match them to situations, get quiet voices heard, sort persuasion from pressure and lead a team with trust and effort meters.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_JbsNfWnYQT":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What does it mean to argue with the idea, not the person?","choices":["Give reasons about the view, not insults about who holds it","Never disagree with anyone","Agree in public and complain in private"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where does your reply aim: at their reasons, or at them?","explain":"Respectful disagreement deals with reasons and evidence. Insults, labels and jokes about the person do not."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"What is a straw man?","choices":["A weak, twisted version of someone's view that is easy to knock down","Their best reason, stated fairly","A question to check what they mean"],"answer":0,"hint":"A man made of straw is easy to knock over.","explain":"A straw man replaces their real view with a silly one. Beating it proves nothing about what they actually think."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Why steelman someone's view before you answer?","choices":["They feel heard and you answer what they really think","It makes your side look weak","It ends the argument at once"],"answer":0,"hint":"How do people react when they feel understood?","explain":"People listen more when they feel understood, and your answer is then about their real reasons, not a guess."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Someone replies to you in capitals: 'YOU ARE SO WRONG.' What is the best next move?","choices":["Ask what part they think is wrong","Reply in bigger capitals","Tag friends to back you up"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which move adds a reason, not more heat?","explain":"A real question turns the heat down and gets to their reason. Shouting back or a pile-on heats it up."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"'Everyone knows nobody reads any more.' What is wrong with this as evidence?","choices":["'Everyone knows' cannot be checked","Nothing, it is a fact","It is too polite"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who measured it, and how?","explain":"'Everyone knows' is a claim, not evidence. A survey with numbers would let people check it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Two friends talk calmly but still disagree. What does that show?","choices":["You can disagree and still respect each other","The talk failed","One of them must be lying"],"answer":0,"hint":"Must every talk have a winner?","explain":"Understanding each other is a good result even without agreement. Respect does not need a winner."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Why look for common ground in a disagreement?","choices":["It shows what you agree on, so you argue about less","It means you give up your view","It is a trick to win"],"answer":0,"hint":"What happens to the size of the disagreement?","explain":"Shared goals, like both wanting students to learn, narrow the argument to the real difference. You still keep your view."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"A thread is at a temperature of {t}. Someone posts a sarcastic meme, which adds 14. What is the temperature now?","answer":"t+14","hint":"Sarcasm adds heat. Add 14 to {t}.","explain":"{t} + 14 = {=t+14}. Mockery adds heat and no reason.","params":{"t":[30,80]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m10","prompt":"A thread is at {t}. You ask a real question (it drops 16), then say what you agree with (it drops 18). What is it now?","answer":"t-34","hint":"Both moves lower the heat. Take away 16, then 18.","explain":"{t} − 16 − 18 = {=t-34}. Questions and shared ground both cool a thread.","params":{"t":[50,90]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which reply to 'uniform should be scrapped' is respectful disagreement?","choices":["I get the cost point, but uniform stops pressure over brands.","Only show-offs want that.","lol no"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which reply notes their point and gives a reason?","explain":"It accepts their real point (cost) and gives a reason against. The other two attack or dismiss the person."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Someone says school should start later. Which reply challenges the idea?","choices":["Buses and parents' work times would need to change too.","Only lazy people want a lie-in.","You always say stupid things."],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one is about school start times at all?","explain":"The first reply raises a real problem with the idea. The other two attack the person and say nothing about start times."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Which is a steelman of 'phones should be banned in school'?","choices":["Phones split attention, and lessons go better without them.","You just hate people having fun.","Some teachers do not like phones."],"answer":0,"hint":"Which summary would the other person agree is their real reason?","explain":"A steelman states their best reason fairly. 'You hate fun' is a straw man, and 'some teachers' misses the point."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A thread is at a temperature of {t}. Asking a real question lowers it by 16. What is it now?","answer":"t-16","hint":"A question lowers the heat. Take 16 away from {t}.","explain":"{t} − 16 = {=t-16}. Questions show you are listening, so the heat drops.","params":{"t":[50,90]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"In a thread of {n} replies, 3 attack the person. How many replies are left that could deal with the idea?","answer":"n-3","hint":"Take the 3 attacks away from all {n} replies.","explain":"{n} − 3 = {=n-3} replies could be about the idea. The 3 personal attacks add nothing to the argument.","params":{"n":[6,15]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which gives the strongest support for a point?","choices":["A named survey of thousands of people","A story about your best friend","Writing the point in capitals"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one could a doubter check for themselves?","explain":"A named survey can be checked. One story is not a pattern, and capitals add volume, not evidence."}],"explain":{"kid":["Disagreeing is normal. Respect means you argue with the idea, not the person. A reply about the idea gives a reason or asks a question. A reply that calls someone lazy, stupid or too old says nothing about the idea at all.","Before you answer, steelman their view. That means saying their best reason the way they would say it. If they reply, yes, that is what I mean, you have it right. The opposite is a straw man: a silly, weak version that is easy to knock down. It feels good but it does not convince anyone.","Back your point with something others can check, such as a named survey or study. One story about a cousin is not a pattern. Capitals make a reply louder, not truer. And it is fair to ask who did the survey.","Online, things heat up fast. Questions, saying what you agree with, and taking a break all cool a thread down. Capitals, sarcasm and tagging friends to pile on heat it up. You may still disagree at the end. That is fine, if you both listened."],"adult":["Ages 14 to 16. The PSHE Association Programme of Study (KS4) covers how to talk with respect and handle conflict, online too. The DfE guidance on Relationships, Sex and Health Education says pupils should know how to disagree with respect and how what they post affects others. This lesson turns that into skills teens can practise.","Steelmanning comes from what are often called Rapoport's rules, made well known by Daniel Dennett. First restate the other view so well that they wish they had put it that way. Then say where you agree. Only then argue. Studies of online talk find that rude comments make readers more hostile and less open, while questions and shared values lower the heat. The thread meter in the lab is a model of that pattern, not a real scale.","Common mistakes: thinking respect means never disagreeing, or that a calm talk must end with a winner. Attacking a straw man. Treating one vivid story as proof. Taking volume (capitals, pile-ons, sarcasm) for strength. Assuming the other side has bad motives. Finding shared goals first does not mean giving in. It just shrinks what you really disagree about."]},"familyTip":"Pick a light topic you disagree on at home, like pineapple on pizza or the best film. Before either of you argues, each must sum up the other's best reason until the other says 'yes, that's it'. Notice how the talk feels different. Then look at a comment section together and spot replies that attack the idea and replies that attack the person.","keyIdea":"You can disagree strongly and still respect the person. Argue with the idea, not the person. Before you answer, steelman their view: say their best reason the way they would say it. Back your point with something others can check, not one story or a louder voice. Online, slow down: questions, shared ground and a pause cool a thread, while capitals, pile-ons and sarcasm heat it up. A calm talk can end with you still disagreeing, and that is fine.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does each reply challenge the idea or attack the person? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["challenges the idea","attacks the person"],"items":[["What evidence is that based on?",0],["Typical of someone your age.",1],["I see your point, but the cost is too high.",0],["Nobody cares what you think.",1],["Could that work in a small school too?",0],["You are just jealous.",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagdisag","caption":"Flagship lab: sort idea from person, steelman three views, back a point with evidence, cool a heated thread and find common ground.","opts":{"step":0}}}});
